window.PYQ_DATA.add({"id":"px-ncert11-phy-gravitation","kind":"practice","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"title":"Gravitation · Practice","chapter":"Gravitation","subject":"Physics","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","marking":{"mcq":[4,-1]},"rules":{},"note":"","questions":[{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-001","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":1,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"A comet moves around the Sun in a highly elongated elliptical orbit. Its distance from the Sun is 0.5 AU at perihelion and 31.5 AU at aphelion. Taking the Earth's orbit as a circle of radius 1 AU with a period of 1 year, what is the comet's period?","image":"","options":["16 years","22.6 years","64 years","176.8 years"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: $r_P$ = 0.5 AU and $r_A$ = 31.5 AU.\nThe semi-major axis is half of AP: $a = \\frac{r_P + r_A}{2} = \\frac{0.5 + 31.5}{2} = 16$ AU.\nFormula: $T = T_E \\left(\\frac{a}{a_E}\\right)^{3/2}$.\nSubstituting: $T = 16^{3/2} \\times 1 = 64$ years.\nUsing the aphelion distance instead of a wrongly gives 176.8 years.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-002","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":2,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"In a Cavendish-type torsion balance, a light bar of length $L = 0.60$ m carries a small lead sphere of mass $m = 1.0$ kg at each end. A large lead sphere of mass $M = 100$ kg is placed near each small sphere, on opposite sides, so that the centre-to-centre distance is $d = 0.10$ m in each case. The suspension wire has a restoring torque per unit angle of twist $\\tau = 2.0 \\times 10^{-5}$ N m rad$^{-1}$. Take $G = 6.67 \\times 10^{-11}$ N m$^2$ kg$^{-2}$. Find the angle through which the wire twists at equilibrium.","image":"","options":["0.020 rad","0.010 rad","0.040 rad","0.033 rad"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $M = 100$ kg, $m = 1.0$ kg, $d = 0.10$ m, $L = 0.60$ m, $\\tau = 2.0\\times10^{-5}$ N m rad$^{-1}$.\nFormula: the two equal and opposite forces form a couple of torque $FL$. At equilibrium, $G\\frac{Mm}{d^2}L = \\tau\\theta$.\nThe force between each pair of spheres is $F = \\frac{6.67\\times10^{-11}\\times100\\times1}{0.01} = 6.67\\times10^{-7}$ N.\nSubstituting: $\\theta = \\frac{FL}{\\tau} = \\frac{6.67\\times10^{-7}\\times0.60}{2.0\\times10^{-5}} \\approx 0.020$ rad.\nThe option 0.033 rad comes from leaving out $L$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-003","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":3,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: For a planet in an elliptical orbit around the Sun, its angular speed about the Sun is greatest at perihelion.\nStatement II: The linear momentum of the planet remains constant throughout its orbit, since the Sun's force on it is central.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true.","Both Statement I and Statement II are false.","Statement I is true but Statement II is false.","Statement I is false but Statement II is true."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I: angular momentum $L = m r^2 \\omega$ is conserved under the central gravitational force. So $\\omega \\propto \\frac{1}{r^2}$, which is largest where r is smallest, at perihelion. True.\nStatement II: a central force exerts zero torque, so angular momentum is conserved. However, the force itself is not zero, so it continuously changes the planet's velocity. NCERT notes that linear momentum is not conserved. False.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-004","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":4,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"At a certain height above the earth's surface, the weight of a body is 36% less than its weight on the surface. Taking $R_E$ = 6400 km, find this height.","image":"","options":["1152 km","3600 km","1600 km","4267 km"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: $\\frac{W(h)}{W} = 0.64$, $R_E$ = 6400 km.\nFormula: $\\frac{g(h)}{g} = \\left(\\frac{R_E}{R_E+h}\\right)^2$. The binomial form cannot be used here because the change is not small.\nSubstituting: $\\frac{R_E}{R_E+h} = 0.8$, so $R_E + h = 1.25R_E$ and $h = 0.25R_E$.\nSo h = 1600 km. The value 1152 km comes from wrongly applying $\\frac{2h}{R_E} = 0.36$.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-005","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":5,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"A 100 kg body is raised from the earth's surface to a height equal to the earth's radius. Take $R_E = 6.4\\times10^{6}$ m and $g = 10$ m/s$^2$ at the surface. What is the increase in the gravitational potential energy of the body?","image":"","options":["$6.4\\times10^{9}$ J","$1.6\\times10^{9}$ J","$2.13\\times10^{9}$ J","$3.2\\times10^{9}$ J"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Given: $m = 100$ kg, $h = R_E = 6.4\\times10^6$ m. Since $h$ is not small, $mgh$ cannot be used.\nFormula: $\\Delta U = -\\frac{GM_Em}{2R_E} + \\frac{GM_Em}{R_E} = \\frac{GM_Em}{2R_E} = \\frac{mgR_E}{2}$, using $GM_E = gR_E^2$.\nSubstituting: $\\Delta U = \\frac{100\\times10\\times6.4\\times10^6}{2}$.\nSo $\\Delta U = 3.2\\times10^{9}$ J. The option $6.4\\times10^9$ J is the incorrect $mgh$ result.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-006","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":6,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the following statements about a geostationary satellite:\n(i) Its period of revolution is 24 hours.\n(ii) Its orbit lies in the equatorial plane of the earth.\n(iii) It revolves from east to west.\n(iv) It appears stationary to an observer on the earth.\nHow many of these statements are correct?","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: its period matches the earth's rotation period of 24 h. \n(ii) Correct: it must orbit in the equatorial plane to stay above one point. \n(iii) Incorrect: it moves west to east, in the same sense as the earth's rotation. \n(iv) Correct: since it keeps pace with the earth's rotation, it appears fixed in the sky. \nSo three statements are correct. \nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-007","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":7,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Kepler's law of areas is a consequence of conservation of angular momentum of the planet about the Sun.\nStatement II: Angular momentum of a body is conserved only when the force on it obeys the inverse square law.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. The areal velocity equals $\\frac{L}{2m}$. Since L is constant, equal areas are swept in equal times.\nStatement II is incorrect. Angular momentum is conserved for any central force, because a central force exerts zero torque about the centre. NCERT notes that this result is not special to the inverse square law.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-008","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":8,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"A thin uniform rod of mass 3 kg and length 2 m lies along the x-axis. A 1 kg point mass is placed on the rod's axis, 1 m beyond its nearer end. Find the gravitational force on the point mass. (Take $G = 6.67\\times10^{-11}$ N m$^2$/kg$^2$.)","image":"","options":["$6.67\\times10^{-11}$ N","$5.00\\times10^{-11}$ N","$20.0\\times10^{-11}$ N","$2.22\\times10^{-11}$ N"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: M = 3 kg, L = 2 m, m = 1 kg, d = 1 m.\nA rod is not spherically symmetric, so we cannot place its mass at the centre. Instead, add the forces of its small elements.\nFormula: $dF = \\frac{Gm(M/L)dx}{x^2}$. Integrating from d to d+L gives $F = \\frac{GMm}{d(d+L)}$.\nSubstituting: $F = \\frac{6.67\\times10^{-11}\\times3\\times1}{1\\times3}$.\nSo F = $6.67\\times10^{-11}$ N.\nThe centre-of-mass guess $\\frac{GMm}{2^2}$ gives the wrong value $5.00\\times10^{-11}$ N.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-009","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":9,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"Two point masses of 10 kg and 20 kg are 0.5 m apart. Take the potential energy to be zero at infinite separation. What is the gravitational potential energy of the pair? ($G = 6.67\\times10^{-11}$ N m$^2$ kg$^{-2}$)","image":"","options":["$-2.67\\times10^{-8}$ J","$-1.33\\times10^{-8}$ J","$-5.34\\times10^{-8}$ J","$+2.67\\times10^{-8}$ J"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $m_1$ = 10 kg, $m_2$ = 20 kg, r = 0.5 m, and U = 0 at infinity.\nFormula: $U = -\\frac{Gm_1m_2}{r}$\nSubstituting: $U = -\\frac{6.67\\times10^{-11}\\times200}{0.5}$\nSo $U = -2.67\\times10^{-8}$ J.\nThe sign must be negative because gravity is attractive. This rules out the positive option.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-010","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":10,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"Four identical point masses of 10 kg each are placed at the corners of a square of side 1 m. What is the magnitude of the net gravitational force on any one of the masses? (Take $G = 6.67 \\times 10^{-11}$ N m$^2$/kg$^2$.)","image":"","options":["$1.28 \\times 10^{-8}$ N","$9.43 \\times 10^{-9}$ N","$1.67 \\times 10^{-8}$ N","$2.33 \\times 10^{-8}$ N"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $m = 10$ kg, $a = 1$ m, so $F_0 = \\frac{Gm^2}{a^2} = 6.67 \\times 10^{-9}$ N\nThe two adjacent masses each exert $F_0$ at 90° to each other. Their resultant is $\\sqrt{2}F_0$ along the diagonal.\nThe diagonal mass is at distance $\\sqrt{2}a$, so it exerts $\\frac{F_0}{2}$ along the same diagonal.\nNet force $= \\left(\\sqrt{2} + \\frac{1}{2}\\right)F_0 = 1.914 \\times 6.67 \\times 10^{-9} \\approx 1.28 \\times 10^{-8}$ N\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-011","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":11,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The net gravitational force exerted by a uniform hollow spherical shell on a point mass placed anywhere inside it is zero.\nStatement II: A point mass placed inside a uniform hollow spherical shell is shielded from the gravitational forces of bodies lying outside the shell.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is true. Forces from different regions of the shell cancel completely for any point inside it.\nStatement II is false. The shell only produces no force of its own. Bodies outside the shell still attract the particle inside.\nUnlike a metallic shell, which shields electrical forces, gravitational shielding is not possible.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-012","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":12,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): An astronaut inside a satellite orbiting the earth experiences weightlessness.\nReason (R): At the height of the satellite, the gravitational force of the earth on the astronaut is zero.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true. An astronaut in an orbiting satellite does experience weightlessness.\nR is false. The earth's gravity at that height is only slightly less than g, and it is far from zero. This gravity supplies the centripetal force that keeps the satellite in orbit.\nWeightlessness arises because the astronaut and the satellite are both in free fall towards the earth with the same acceleration. So the floor exerts no normal force on the astronaut.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-013","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":13,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"Take the earth to be a uniform sphere of radius 6400 km. At what height above the surface is the acceleration due to gravity equal to its value at a depth of 3200 km? Use the exact expression for g at height.","image":"","options":["≈ 2651 km","1600 km","3200 km","9051 km"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: d = 3200 km = R/2, so at that depth $g(d) = g/2$.\nFormula: at height h, exactly, $g(h) = \\frac{gR^2}{(R+h)^2}$.\nSetting $\\frac{R^2}{(R+h)^2} = \\frac{1}{2}$ gives $R + h = \\sqrt{2}R$.\nSubstituting: $h = (\\sqrt{2} - 1) \\times 6400 \\approx 2651$ km.\nThe value 1600 km comes from the approximation $(1 - 2h/R)$, which is not valid when h is large.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-014","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":14,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): If the zero of gravitational potential energy is moved from infinity to the surface of the earth, the gravitational force on a body near the earth does not change.\nReason (R): The potential energy expression contains an arbitrary additive constant, and only differences of potential energy have physical meaning.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT notes that choosing where the potential energy is zero does not alter the gravitational force.\nR is true. The general form is $V = -\\frac{Gm_1m_2}{r}$ + constant, and the constant can take any value.\nWhy R explains A: force and work depend only on changes in potential energy. Adding a constant changes no difference, so the force stays the same.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-015","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":15,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"A satellite of mass m moves in an elliptical orbit around the earth (mass M). Its distances from the earth's centre at the nearest and farthest points are $r_1$ and $r_2$. What is its kinetic energy at the nearest point?","image":"","options":["$\\frac{GMm}{r_1 + r_2}$","$\\frac{GMm\\, r_1}{r_2 (r_1 + r_2)}$","$\\frac{GMm}{2r_1}$","$\\frac{GMm\\, r_2}{r_1 (r_1 + r_2)}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"The semi-major axis is half of the line joining the nearest and farthest points: $2a = r_1 + r_2$.\nTotal energy: $E = -\\frac{GMm}{2a} = -\\frac{GMm}{r_1 + r_2}$.\nAt the nearest point, PE = $-\\frac{GMm}{r_1}$.\nKE = E − PE = $GMm\\left(\\frac{1}{r_1} - \\frac{1}{r_1 + r_2}\\right) = \\frac{GMm\\, r_2}{r_1(r_1 + r_2)}$.\nOption (3) applies only to a circular orbit. Option (2) is the KE at the farthest point.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-016","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":16,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): In Cavendish's experiment, the gravitational force between a big lead sphere and its neighbouring small lead sphere can be calculated as if each sphere's mass were concentrated at its centre.\nReason (R): A uniform spherical shell attracts a point mass outside it as if the entire mass of the shell were concentrated at its centre.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"R is true: this is the shell result stated in NCERT section 7.3.\nA is true: a uniform solid sphere can be treated as a set of concentric shells.\nEach shell acts on an outside point as if its mass were at the common centre, so the whole sphere does too.\nThat is why NCERT writes $F = \\frac{GMm}{d^2}$ with d measured between the centres. So R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-017","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":17,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"A satellite of mass 600 kg revolves around Earth in a circular orbit of radius $2R_E$. Find the minimum energy required to shift it to a circular orbit of radius $3R_E$. Take $R_E = 6.4 \\times 10^6$ m and g = 10 m/s$^2$ at Earth's surface.","image":"","options":["$3.2 \\times 10^9$ J","$6.4 \\times 10^9$ J","$1.6 \\times 10^9$ J","$9.6 \\times 10^9$ J"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: m = 600 kg, $r_1 = 2R_E$, $r_2 = 3R_E$, and $GM_E = gR_E^2$.\nFormula: For a circular orbit, $E = -\\frac{GM_Em}{2r}$. The energy needed is $\\Delta E = GM_Em\\left(\\frac{1}{4R_E}-\\frac{1}{6R_E}\\right) = \\frac{mgR_E}{12}$.\nSubstituting: $\\Delta E = \\frac{600 \\times 10 \\times 6.4\\times10^6}{12}$.\nSo $\\Delta E = 3.2 \\times 10^9$ J.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-018","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":18,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"The earth is an approximate sphere. If the interior contained matter which is not of the same density everywhere, then on the surface of the earth, the acceleration due to gravity","image":"","options":["will be directed towards the centre but not the same everywhere.","will have the same value everywhere but not directed towards the centre.","will be same everywhere in magnitude directed towards the centre.","cannot be zero at any point."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Key idea: With non-uniform density, the earth's mass is no longer spherically symmetric.\nSo surface $g$ can vary in magnitude from place to place and need not point exactly towards the centre. This rules out (1), (2) and (3).\nAt any surface point, all of the earth's mass lies on one side of that point. Its pulls therefore always add up to a net inward-type force and can never cancel completely.\nSo $g$ cannot be zero anywhere on the surface.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Physics Class 11, Ch 8 (MCQ Q8.1)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-019","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":19,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) The escape speed from Earth's surface does not depend on the mass of the projectile.\n(ii) Ignoring air resistance, the escape speed from Earth's surface depends on the direction in which the body is projected.\n(iii) An astronaut in an orbiting satellite feels weightless because gravity is negligible at that height.\n(iv) The orbital speed of an Earth satellite decreases as its height above the surface increases.\n(v) The expression mgh for potential energy is an approximation to the difference in gravitational potential energy between two points.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct. Escape speed is $v_e = \\sqrt{2GM_E/R_E}$, which contains no projectile mass.\n(ii) Incorrect. The energy condition involves only the speed, so escape speed does not depend on direction.\n(iii) Incorrect. Gravity is not small there. The astronaut and satellite are both in free fall, which causes weightlessness.\n(iv) Correct. $V^2 = \\frac{GM_E}{R_E+h}$, so V falls as h rises.\n(v) Correct. NCERT states this directly.\nSo 3 statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-020","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":20,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"Phobos, a moon of Mars, moves in a nearly circular orbit of radius $9.4 \\times 10^{6}$ m with a period of 7 h 39 min. The radius of Mars is $3.4 \\times 10^{6}$ m. Using only these data, find the acceleration due to gravity at the surface of Mars.","image":"","options":["0.49 m/s$^2$","1.87 m/s$^2$","3.74 m/s$^2$","7.48 m/s$^2$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: $r = 9.4 \\times 10^6$ m, $T = 459 \\times 60 = 27540$ s, $R = 3.4 \\times 10^6$ m.\nFormula: Kepler's third law gives $GM = \\frac{4\\pi^2 r^3}{T^2}$, and the surface gravity is $g = \\frac{GM}{R^2}$.\nSubstituting: $GM = \\frac{39.48 \\times 8.31 \\times 10^{20}}{7.58 \\times 10^8} \\approx 4.32 \\times 10^{13}$ m$^3$/s$^2$.\nThen $g = \\frac{4.32 \\times 10^{13}}{1.156 \\times 10^{13}} \\approx 3.74$ m/s$^2$.\nDividing by $r^2$ instead of $R^2$ would give 0.49 m/s$^2$, which is the acceleration of Phobos, not the surface value.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-021","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":21,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"The escape speed from the Earth's surface is 11.2 km/s. What is the minimum speed with which a body must be projected from a point at a height equal to the Earth's radius $R_E$ above the surface, so that it escapes the Earth's gravity?","image":"","options":["5.6 km/s","11.2 km/s","15.8 km/s","7.92 km/s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Given: $h = R_E$, so the distance from the Earth's centre is $2R_E$; $v_e = 11.2$ km/s.\nFormula: $\\frac{1}{2}mv^2 = \\frac{GM_E m}{R_E + h}$ gives $v = \\sqrt{\\frac{2GM_E}{R_E+h}}$.\nSubstituting: $v = \\sqrt{\\frac{2GM_E}{2R_E}} = \\frac{v_e}{\\sqrt{2}} = \\frac{11.2}{1.414}$.\nSo $v \\approx 7.92$ km/s.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-022","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":22,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I:\n(A) Escape speed from Earth's surface\n(B) Period of a satellite orbiting very close to Earth's surface\n(C) Escape speed from the Moon's surface\n(D) Period of revolution of the Moon around Earth\nList-II:\n(I) about 85 minutes\n(II) about 11.2 km/s\n(III) about 27.3 days\n(IV) about 2.3 km/s","image":"","options":["A-II, B-I, C-IV, D-III","A-II, B-III, C-IV, D-I","A-IV, B-I, C-II, D-III","A-II, B-I, C-III, D-IV"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) Earth's escape speed is $\\sqrt{2gR_E}$, which is about 11.2 km/s, so A matches II.\n(B) For a satellite close to the surface, $T_0 = 2\\pi\\sqrt{R_E/g}$, which is about 85 min, so B matches I.\n(C) The Moon's escape speed is about 2.3 km/s, so C matches IV.\n(D) The Moon's period is about 27.3 days, so D matches III.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-023","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":23,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"A Cavendish torsion balance is rebuilt with every linear dimension (radii of the big and small spheres, the separation d and the bar length L) multiplied by a factor k. The spheres are made of the same lead, and the same suspension wire is used. The equilibrium angle of twist becomes proportional to:","image":"","options":["$k^3$","$k^4$","$k^5$","$k^7$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"At equilibrium, $\\theta = \\frac{GMmL}{\\tau d^2}$.\nThe density is unchanged, so each mass scales as volume: $M \\propto k^3$ and $m \\propto k^3$.\nThe bar length scales as $L \\propto k$, and $d^2 \\propto k^2$. The wire is the same, so $\\tau$ is unchanged.\nTherefore $\\theta \\propto \\frac{k^3\\cdot k^3\\cdot k}{k^2} = k^5$.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-024","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":24,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"Two uniform solid spheres, each of radius $R$, have masses $M$ and $16M$. They are held fixed with a centre-to-centre separation of $10R$. A particle is projected from the surface of the lighter sphere directly towards the centre of the heavier sphere. What is the minimum projection speed needed for the particle to reach the surface of the heavier sphere?","image":"","options":["$\\sqrt{\\frac{5GM}{9R}}$","$\\sqrt{\\frac{3GM}{5R}}$","$\\sqrt{\\frac{2GM}{R}}$","$\\sqrt{\\frac{GM}{2R}}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Neutral point at distance $r$ from the centre of $M$: $\\frac{GM}{r^2} = \\frac{16GM}{(10R-r)^2}$, so $10R - r = 4r$ and $r = 2R$.\nThe particle only needs to reach this point with near-zero speed; beyond it, the pull of $16M$ takes over.\nInitial energy per unit mass: $\\frac{v^2}{2} - \\frac{GM}{R} - \\frac{16GM}{9R}$.\nEnergy per unit mass at the neutral point: $-\\frac{GM}{2R} - \\frac{16GM}{8R}$.\nEquating: $\\frac{v^2}{2} = \\frac{GM}{R}\\left(1 + \\frac{16}{9} - \\frac{1}{2} - 2\\right) = \\frac{5GM}{18R}$, so $v = \\sqrt{\\frac{5GM}{9R}}$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-025","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":25,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"Using $g = 9.8$ m s$^{-2}$, $R_E = 6.4\\times10^{6}$ m and $G = 6.67\\times10^{-11}$ N m$^2$ kg$^{-2}$, estimate the mass of the earth.","image":"","options":["$9.4\\times10^{17}$ kg","$6.0\\times10^{24}$ kg","$1.2\\times10^{25}$ kg","$2.4\\times10^{25}$ kg"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: g = 9.8 m s$^{-2}$, $R_E = 6.4\\times10^6$ m, $G = 6.67\\times10^{-11}$ N m$^2$ kg$^{-2}$.\nFormula: $g = \\frac{GM_E}{R_E^2}$, so $M_E = \\frac{gR_E^2}{G}$.\nSubstituting: $M_E = \\frac{9.8\\times(6.4\\times10^6)^2}{6.67\\times10^{-11}} = \\frac{4.01\\times10^{14}}{6.67\\times10^{-11}}$.\nSo $M_E \\approx 6.0\\times10^{24}$ kg. Option (1) comes from wrongly using $R_E$ instead of $R_E^2$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-026","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":26,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"A uniform solid sphere of mass 40 kg and radius 0.2 m is surrounded by a concentric thin uniform shell of mass 60 kg and radius 0.5 m. A small mass m is placed first at 0.1 m from the centre (inside a narrow tunnel in the sphere) and then at 0.4 m from the centre. Find the ratio $F_{0.1}/F_{0.4}$ of the gravitational forces on m at the two positions.","image":"","options":["2","0.8","16","0.5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The outer shell (0.5 m) encloses both points, so it exerts zero force at each.\nAt r = 0.1 m (inside the solid sphere): $F_1 = \\frac{GMmr}{R^3} = Gm\\frac{40\\times0.1}{0.2^3} = 500\\,Gm$.\nAt r = 0.4 m (outside the sphere): $F_2 = \\frac{GMm}{r^2} = Gm\\frac{40}{0.16} = 250\\,Gm$.\nSo the ratio = 500/250 = 2.\nWrongly adding the shell's 60 kg at 0.4 m gives 0.8.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-027","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":27,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"A body weighs 600 N on the surface of the earth. Assume the earth is a sphere of uniform density. What is its weight in a mine at a depth equal to one-fourth of the earth's radius?","image":"","options":["450 N","337.5 N","300 N","600 N"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $W_0 = 600$ N and $d = \\frac{R_E}{4}$.\nFormula: $g(d) = g\\left(1 - \\frac{d}{R_E}\\right)$, so $W(d) = W_0\\left(1 - \\frac{d}{R_E}\\right)$.\nSubstituting: $W = 600 \\times \\left(1 - \\frac{1}{4}\\right) = 600 \\times 0.75$.\nSo the weight at that depth is 450 N.\nOption (2) wrongly squares the factor (0.75 squared), as if g fell off like the inverse-square law outside the earth.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-028","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":28,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"A planet moves in an elliptical orbit of eccentricity 0.5 with period 12 years. The Sun S is at the focus nearer to the perihelion P. B and C are the two ends of the minor axis. How long does the planet take to travel from B to C along the half of the orbit that contains the aphelion A?","image":"","options":["4.09 years","6.00 years","7.91 years","9.00 years"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"By the law of areas, time is proportional to the area swept by the radius vector from S.\nThe area swept from B via A to C equals half the ellipse plus triangle SBC.\nTriangle SBC has base 2b and height ae (the distance from the centre to S), so its area is $abe$.\nFraction of the period: $\\frac{\\frac{\\pi ab}{2} + abe}{\\pi ab} = \\frac{1}{2} + \\frac{e}{\\pi}$.\nSubstituting: $t = 12\\left(0.5 + \\frac{0.5}{\\pi}\\right) \\approx 7.91$ years. The value 4.09 years is the time for the perihelion half.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-029","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":29,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): A planet moving in an elliptical orbit around the Sun moves faster at perihelion than at aphelion.\nReason (R): The gravitational force on the planet is directed towards the Sun, so the planet's angular momentum about the Sun stays constant.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. Kepler's law of areas states that planets move faster when nearer the Sun, and perihelion is the nearest point.\nR is true. A central force exerts no torque about the Sun, so angular momentum $L = mvr$ (at P and A) is conserved.\nR explains A. With constant L, a smaller r gives a larger v, which is the relation $v_P r_P = v_A r_A$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-030","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":30,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) The acceleration due to gravity at a height h does not depend on the mass of the body placed there.\n(ii) For $h \\ll R_E$, the fractional decrease in g at height h is approximately $\\frac{2h}{R_E}$.\n(iii) If $h = R_E$ is substituted into $g\\left(1 - \\frac{2h}{R_E}\\right)$, the result is negative, though the true value is $\\frac{g}{4}$.\n(iv) Above the surface, g varies inversely as the square of the height h.\n(v) For small heights, doubling h roughly doubles the decrease in g.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: $g(h) = \\frac{GM_E}{(R_E+h)^2}$ contains no mass of the body.\n(ii) Correct: $\\frac{g - g(h)}{g} \\approx \\frac{2h}{R_E}$.\n(iii) Correct: the formula gives g(1 − 2) = −g, while the exact formula gives $\\frac{g}{4}$.\n(iv) Incorrect: g ∝ $\\frac{1}{(R_E+h)^2}$, the inverse square of distance from the centre, not of h.\n(v) Correct: the decrease $\\frac{2gh}{R_E}$ is linear in h.\nFour statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-031","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":31,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"Four particles, each of mass m, sit at the corners of a square of side l. A fifth particle, also of mass m, is brought slowly from infinity to the centre of the square. What is the total gravitational potential energy of the five-particle system? Take zero potential energy at infinity.","image":"","options":["$-(4+5\\sqrt{2})\\frac{Gm^2}{l}$","$-(4+\\sqrt{2})\\frac{Gm^2}{l}$","$-(8+\\sqrt{2})\\frac{Gm^2}{l}$","$-(4+3\\sqrt{2})\\frac{Gm^2}{l}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Step 1: The original four masses have $U_1 = -(4+\\sqrt{2})\\frac{Gm^2}{l}$ (4 side pairs plus 2 diagonal pairs).\nStep 2: The centre is $\\frac{l}{\\sqrt{2}}$ from each corner. The potential there is $V = -4\\frac{Gm}{l/\\sqrt{2}} = -4\\sqrt{2}\\frac{Gm}{l}$.\nStep 3: The new particle adds 4 more pairs, contributing $mV = -4\\sqrt{2}\\frac{Gm^2}{l}$.\nStep 4: Total $U = -(4+5\\sqrt{2})\\frac{Gm^2}{l} \\approx -11.07\\frac{Gm^2}{l}$.\nOption (4) wrongly uses the full diagonal $\\sqrt{2}l$ as the centre-to-corner distance.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-032","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":32,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"In Cavendish's experiment, two large lead spheres are placed near the two small lead spheres on the suspended bar, on opposite sides. What do the big spheres exert on the bar?","image":"","options":["A net force towards the big spheres but no torque","No net force, only a torque about the suspension wire","Both a net force and a torque","Neither a net force nor a torque, because the forces are equal and opposite"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Each big sphere attracts its neighbouring small sphere with a force F.\nThe two forces are equal and opposite and act at the two ends of the bar, so the net force on the bar is zero.\nThey do not act along the same line, so they form a couple of torque F × L, where L is the bar length.\nThis torque twists the wire. Option (4) wrongly assumes that equal and opposite forces always give zero torque.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-033","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":33,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Kepler's law of areas is a consequence of the conservation of angular momentum of the planet about the Sun.\nStatement II: This conservation of angular momentum holds only because gravity obeys an inverse-square law.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct: a constant areal velocity follows directly from constant angular momentum about the Sun.\nStatement II is incorrect: angular momentum is conserved for any central force, because a central force exerts no torque about the centre. It is not special to the inverse-square law.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-034","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":34,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"For a satellite in a circular orbit of radius r around the earth, match List-I with List-II.\nList-I:\n(a) Orbital speed v\n(b) Time period T\n(c) Kinetic energy K\n(d) Angular speed $\\omega$\nList-II:\n(p) $\\propto r^{-1}$\n(q) $\\propto r^{-1/2}$\n(r) $\\propto r^{-3/2}$\n(s) $\\propto r^{3/2}$","image":"","options":["a-q, b-s, c-p, d-r","a-q, b-r, c-p, d-s","a-p, b-s, c-q, d-r","a-r, b-s, c-p, d-q"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) $v = \\sqrt{GM_E/r}$, so $v \\propto r^{-1/2}$ → (q). \n(b) $T = 2\\pi r^{3/2}/\\sqrt{GM_E}$, so $T \\propto r^{3/2}$ → (s). \n(c) $K = \\frac{GM_Em}{2r}$, so $K \\propto r^{-1}$ → (p). \n(d) $\\omega = v/r \\propto r^{-3/2}$ → (r). \nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-035","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":35,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"A satellite of mass m moves in a circular orbit of radius r around the Earth (mass M, radius R). Take potential energy to be zero at infinity. Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(A) Kinetic energy of the satellite\n(B) Potential energy of the satellite\n(C) Total mechanical energy of the satellite\n(D) Minimum kinetic energy needed for mass m to escape from the Earth's surface\nList-II\n(i) $-\\frac{GMm}{r}$\n(ii) $\\frac{GMm}{2r}$\n(iii) $-\\frac{GMm}{2r}$\n(iv) $\\frac{GMm}{R}$","image":"","options":["A-(ii), B-(i), C-(iii), D-(iv)","A-(ii), B-(iii), C-(i), D-(iv)","A-(iv), B-(i), C-(iii), D-(ii)","A-(iii), B-(i), C-(ii), D-(iv)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) Using $v^2 = GM/r$, KE = $\\frac{GMm}{2r}$, so A matches (ii).\n(B) PE = $-\\frac{GMm}{r}$, so B matches (i).\n(C) Total energy = KE + PE = $-\\frac{GMm}{2r}$, so C matches (iii).\n(D) From $\\frac{1}{2}mv_e^2 = \\frac{GMm}{R}$, D matches (iv).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-036","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":36,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about earth satellites are correct?\n(i) Kepler's law $T^2 = k(R_E+h)^3$ also holds for an elliptical orbit if $(R_E + h)$ is replaced by the semi-major axis.\n(ii) The period of a satellite in a given circular orbit does not depend on the satellite's mass.\n(iii) The moon's period of revolution, about 27.3 days, is roughly equal to its period of rotation about its own axis.\n(iv) A satellite in a higher circular orbit has a greater orbital speed.\n(v) A geostationary satellite revolves in the equatorial plane with a period of 24 hours.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: NCERT notes this for elliptical orbits, with the earth at one focus.\n(ii) Correct: $T = \\frac{2\\pi (R_E+h)^{3/2}}{\\sqrt{GM_E}}$ contains no satellite mass.\n(iii) Correct: NCERT states the two periods are roughly equal.\n(iv) Incorrect: $V = \\sqrt{\\frac{GM_E}{R_E+h}}$ decreases with height.\n(v) Correct: a geostationary satellite moves in the equatorial plane in the same sense as the earth's rotation, with $T = 24$ h.\nFour statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-037","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":37,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"Using g = 9.8 m/s$^2$, $R_E = 6.4 \\times 10^6$ m and $G = 6.67 \\times 10^{-11}$ N m$^2$/kg$^2$, estimate the mass of the earth.","image":"","options":["$6.0 \\times 10^{24}$ kg","$3.0 \\times 10^{24}$ kg","$12.0 \\times 10^{24}$ kg","$0.96 \\times 10^{24}$ kg"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: g = 9.8 m/s$^2$, $R_E = 6.4\\times10^6$ m, G = $6.67\\times10^{-11}$ SI units.\nFormula: $g = \\frac{GM_E}{R_E^2}$, so $M_E = \\frac{gR_E^2}{G}$.\nSubstituting: $M_E = \\frac{9.8 \\times 4.096\\times10^{13}}{6.67\\times10^{-11}}$.\nSo $M_E \\approx 6.0\\times10^{24}$ kg. This is why it is said that \"Cavendish weighed the earth.\"\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-038","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":38,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"A planet moves around the sun in a circular orbit whose radius is 2.25 times the earth's orbital radius. Taking the earth's year as 365 days, the length of a year on this planet is about","image":"","options":["1231.9 days","821.3 days","547.5 days","1847.8 days"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $R_P/R_E = 2.25$ and $T_E = 365$ days. \nFormula (Kepler's third law): $\\frac{T_P^2}{T_E^2} = \\frac{R_P^3}{R_E^3}$, so $T_P = T_E\\,(2.25)^{3/2}$ \nSubstituting: $(2.25)^{3/2} = 1.5^3 = 3.375$, so $T_P = 3.375\\times365$ \nSo $T_P \\approx 1231.9$ days. \nUsing $(2.25)^2$ instead of $(2.25)^{3/2}$ gives the wrong value 1847.8 days. \nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-039","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":39,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about Cavendish's experiment is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["Moving the big spheres to the opposite side of the small spheres reverses the direction of the torque on the bar","The restoring couple per unit twist of the wire can be found separately by applying a known torque and measuring the twist","The bar turns because a net gravitational force pulls the whole bar towards the big spheres","The wire keeps twisting until the restoring torque of the wire equals the gravitational torque"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: when the big spheres are shifted to the other side, the torque reverses and the bar rotates the other way.\n(2) Correct: NCERT says $\\tau$ can be measured independently in exactly this way.\n(3) Incorrect: the two attractive forces are equal and opposite, so the net force on the bar is zero. Only a torque acts.\n(4) Correct: equilibrium is reached when $\\tau\\theta$ equals the gravitational torque.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-040","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":40,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["A uniform hollow spherical shell exerts no net gravitational force on a point mass placed anywhere inside it.","A uniform hollow spherical shell shields a particle inside it from the gravitational forces of bodies outside the shell.","For a point mass outside a uniform spherical shell, the shell attracts it as if the entire mass of the shell were at its centre.","The gravitational force between two finite rigid bodies need not act along the line joining their centres of mass."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: forces from the different regions of the shell cancel completely for a point inside.\n(2) Incorrect: the shell's own force on the particle is zero, but it does not block forces from outside bodies. Unlike a metallic shell for electric forces, gravitational shielding is not possible.\n(3) Correct: this is the shell theorem for an external point.\n(4) Correct: only spherically symmetric bodies give a central force on an external particle.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-041","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":41,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: An astronaut in a satellite orbiting the earth feels weightless because the earth's gravitational force at that height is negligible.\nStatement II: The astronaut and the satellite are both in free fall towards the earth with the same acceleration.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is incorrect. At typical satellite heights, g is still a large fraction of its surface value. Gravity is what supplies the centripetal force for the orbit.\nStatement II is correct. NCERT explains that weightlessness arises because the astronaut and the satellite both fall freely towards the earth. Neither presses against the other, so there is no normal reaction.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-042","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":42,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"At what height above the earth's surface does the acceleration due to gravity become one-ninth of its surface value? Take $R_E$ = 6400 km.","image":"","options":["6400 km","19200 km","51200 km","12800 km"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Given: $g(h) = \\frac{g}{9}$, $R_E$ = 6400 km.\nFormula: $\\frac{g(h)}{g} = \\left(\\frac{R_E}{R_E+h}\\right)^2$.\nSubstituting: $\\frac{R_E}{R_E+h} = \\frac{1}{3}$, so $R_E + h = 3R_E$ and $h = 2R_E$.\nSo h = 12800 km. The value 19200 km is the distance from the centre, not the height above the surface.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-043","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":43,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): If the gravitational attraction of the Sun varied as $\\frac{1}{r^3}$ instead of $\\frac{1}{r^2}$, the line joining a planet to the Sun would still sweep out equal areas in equal intervals of time.\nReason (R): The law of areas follows from conservation of angular momentum, which holds for any central force.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A.","A is true but R is false.","A is false but R is true."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Areal velocity is $\\frac{\\Delta A}{\\Delta t} = \\frac{L}{2m}$, so it is constant whenever angular momentum L is constant.\nA central force acts along the line to the Sun, so its torque about the Sun is zero and L is conserved.\nThis does not depend on the force being inverse-square. A $\\frac{1}{r^3}$ central force also conserves L, so A is true.\nR is true and directly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-044","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":44,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"A satellite of mass m revolves around the Earth (mass M) in a circular orbit of radius r with speed v, period T and kinetic energy K. It is shifted to another circular orbit of radius 2r. How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) Its orbital speed becomes $\\frac{v}{\\sqrt{2}}$.\n(ii) Its period becomes $2\\sqrt{2}\\,T$.\n(iii) Its kinetic energy becomes $\\frac{K}{2}$.\n(iv) Its total mechanical energy decreases.\n(v) The minimum energy needed for the transfer is $\\frac{GMm}{4r}$.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: $v=\\sqrt{GM/r}$, so doubling r divides v by $\\sqrt{2}$.\n(ii) Correct: $T\\propto r^{3/2}$, so T becomes $2^{3/2}T=2\\sqrt{2}T$.\n(iii) Correct: $K=\\frac{GMm}{2r}$ is halved.\n(iv) Incorrect: E changes from $-\\frac{GMm}{2r}$ to $-\\frac{GMm}{4r}$. It becomes less negative, so it increases.\n(v) Correct: $\\Delta E=-\\frac{GMm}{4r}+\\frac{GMm}{2r}=\\frac{GMm}{4r}$.\nSo 4 statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-045","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":45,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"Two satellites A and B revolve around the Earth in circular orbits with time periods 8 h and 27 h respectively. What is the ratio of their orbital speeds $v_A / v_B$?","image":"","options":["1.5","0.67","2.25","1.22"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $T_A$ = 8 h, $T_B$ = 27 h.\nKepler's law of periods: $T^2 \\propto r^3$, so $\\frac{r_A}{r_B} = (\\frac{8}{27})^{2/3} = \\frac{4}{9}$.\nOrbital speed: $v^2 = \\frac{GM}{r}$, so $v \\propto r^{-1/2}$.\nSubstituting: $\\frac{v_A}{v_B} = (\\frac{9}{4})^{1/2} = 1.5$.\nThe inner satellite moves faster.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-046","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":46,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"Choose the wrong option.","image":"","options":["Inertial mass is a measure of difficulty of accelerating a body by an external force whereas the gravitational mass is relevant in determining the gravitational force on it by an external mass.","That the gravitational mass and inertial mass are equal is an experimental result.","That the acceleration due to gravity on earth is the same for all bodies is due to the equality of gravitational mass and inertial mass.","Gravitational mass of a particle like proton can depend on the presence of neighbouring heavy objects but the inertial mass cannot."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: Inertial mass measures resistance to acceleration. Gravitational mass sets the gravitational force on a body.\n(2) Correct: The equality of the two masses is an experimental fact, checked to very high precision.\n(3) Correct: In $m_i a = \\frac{GMm_g}{R^2}$, the equality $m_i=m_g$ makes $a$ the same for all bodies.\n(4) Wrong: Gravitational mass is an intrinsic property of a particle, just like inertial mass. It does not change because heavy objects are nearby.\nSo the wrong option is (4).\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Physics Class 11, Ch 8 (MCQ Q8.7)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-047","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":47,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"The escape speed from the earth's surface is 11.2 km/s. What is the minimum speed needed for a body to escape the earth's gravity if it is launched from a height $h = 3R_E$ above the surface? Here $R_E$ is the radius of the earth.","image":"","options":["5.6 km/s","2.8 km/s","3.7 km/s","6.5 km/s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: the launch point is at distance $R_E + h = 4R_E$ from the earth's centre.\nFormula: $v = \\sqrt{\\frac{2GM_E}{R_E + h}}$, so $\\frac{v}{v_e} = \\sqrt{\\frac{R_E}{R_E+h}}$.\nSubstituting: $v = 11.2\\times\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}} = 11.2 \\times \\frac{1}{2}$.\nSo v = 5.6 km/s.\nThe option 2.8 km/s wrongly takes v proportional to $\\frac{1}{R_E+h}$ instead of $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{R_E+h}}$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-048","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":48,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"A satellite is shifted from a lower circular orbit to a higher circular orbit around the earth. How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) Its kinetic energy decreases.\n(ii) Its potential energy increases.\n(iii) Its total mechanical energy increases.\n(iv) Its orbital speed increases.\n(v) Its time period increases.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: KE = $\\frac{GMm}{2r}$ falls as r grows.\n(ii) Correct: PE = $-\\frac{GMm}{r}$ becomes less negative, so it increases.\n(iii) Correct: E = $-\\frac{GMm}{2r}$ becomes less negative. This is why energy must be supplied.\n(iv) Incorrect: $V^2 = \\frac{GM}{r}$, so V decreases.\n(v) Correct: $T^2 \\propto r^3$.\nFour statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-049","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":49,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"The acceleration due to gravity at the surface of the Earth, $g = \\frac{GM_E}{R_E^2}$, does NOT depend on which of the following?","image":"","options":["Mass of the Earth","Radius of the Earth","Universal gravitational constant","Mass of the body falling towards the Earth"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"The force on a body of mass m is $F = \\frac{GM_Em}{R_E^2}$, and Newton's second law gives F = mg.\nThe mass m cancels, so $g = \\frac{GM_E}{R_E^2}$. This involves only G, $M_E$ and $R_E$.\nTherefore g is the same for all bodies, whatever their mass. The other three options all appear in the formula.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-050","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":50,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"A thin uniform spherical shell has mass 50 kg and radius 1 m. A 2 kg point mass is kept at a distance of 3 m from the centre of the shell. Find the gravitational force between them. (Take $G = 6.67\\times10^{-11}$ N m$^2$/kg$^2$.)","image":"","options":["$7.41\\times10^{-10}$ N","$16.7\\times10^{-10}$ N","Zero","$22.2\\times10^{-10}$ N"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: M = 50 kg, m = 2 kg, distance from centre r = 3 m. The point is outside the shell.\nFormula: for an external point the shell acts as if all its mass were at its centre, so $F = \\frac{GMm}{r^2}$.\nSubstituting: $F = \\frac{6.67\\times10^{-11}\\times50\\times2}{3^2}$.\nSo F = $7.41\\times10^{-10}$ N.\nUsing the 2 m distance from the surface instead of the centre gives the wrong value $16.7\\times10^{-10}$ N.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-051","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":51,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"A 10 kg body is raised from the earth's surface to a height equal to the earth's radius. Take $R_E$ = 6400 km and g = 10 m/s$^2$ at the surface. What is the actual increase in its gravitational potential energy?","image":"","options":["$3.2\\times10^{8}$ J","$6.4\\times10^{8}$ J","$1.6\\times10^{8}$ J","$4.8\\times10^{8}$ J"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: m = 10 kg, h = $R_E$ = $6.4\\times10^6$ m, g = 10 m/s$^2$.\nFormula: $\\Delta U = GM_Em\\left(\\frac{1}{R_E}-\\frac{1}{2R_E}\\right) = \\frac{GM_Em}{2R_E} = \\frac{mgR_E}{2}$, using $GM_E = gR_E^2$.\nSubstituting: $\\Delta U = \\frac{10\\times10\\times6.4\\times10^6}{2}$\nSo $\\Delta U = 3.2\\times10^8$ J.\nThe value $6.4\\times10^8$ J comes from using mgh. That expression is only an approximation for $h \\ll R_E$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-052","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":52,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The gravitational force between two finite rigid bodies need not act along the line joining their centres of mass.\nStatement II: For a spherically symmetric body, the gravitational force on a particle outside it acts along the line joining the particle to the body's centre.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I: Correct. The force between two point particles is central. For extended rigid bodies, however, the vector sum over all mass elements need not lie along the line joining the centres of mass.\nStatement II: Correct. For a spherically symmetric body, the sideways components from different parts cancel. The body then acts like a point mass at its centre, so the force is central.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-053","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":53,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"A uniform thin hollow spherical shell has mass 100 kg and radius 1.0 m. A point mass of 2 kg is placed at a distance of 2.0 m from the centre of the shell. What is the magnitude of the gravitational force exerted by the shell on the point mass? (Take $G = 6.67 \\times 10^{-11}$ N m$^2$/kg$^2$.)","image":"","options":["$3.34 \\times 10^{-9}$ N","$1.33 \\times 10^{-8}$ N","$6.67 \\times 10^{-9}$ N","Zero"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: shell mass M = 100 kg, point mass m = 2 kg, distance from the centre r = 2.0 m. The shell radius is R = 1.0 m.\nShell theorem: the point lies outside the shell, so the shell acts as if all its mass were at its centre.\nFormula: $F = \\frac{GMm}{r^2}$, where r is measured from the centre, not from the surface.\nSubstituting: $F = \\frac{6.67\\times10^{-11}\\times100\\times2}{(2.0)^2} = 3.34\\times10^{-9}$ N.\nUsing r − R = 1 m gives $1.33\\times10^{-8}$ N, which is wrong. A force of zero would apply only if the mass were inside the shell.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-054","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":54,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"Point masses of 10 kg each are placed at the three vertices of an equilateral triangle of side 1 m. A point mass of 20 kg is placed at the centroid. What is the magnitude of the net gravitational force on the mass at any one vertex? (Take $G = 6.67 \\times 10^{-11}$ N m$^2$/kg$^2$.)","image":"","options":["$5.16 \\times 10^{-8}$ N","$1.16 \\times 10^{-8}$ N","$2.49 \\times 10^{-8}$ N","$4.00 \\times 10^{-8}$ N"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $m = 10$ kg, $L = 1$ m, so $F_0 = \\frac{Gm^2}{L^2} = 6.67 \\times 10^{-9}$ N\nThe other two vertex masses each exert $F_0$ at 60° to each other. Their resultant is $\\sqrt{3}F_0$, directed along the bisector towards the centroid.\nThe centroid is at distance $\\frac{L}{\\sqrt{3}}$, so the 20 kg mass exerts $\\frac{G(2m)m}{L^2/3} = 6F_0$ in the same direction.\nNet force $= (\\sqrt{3} + 6)F_0 \\approx 7.73 \\times 6.67 \\times 10^{-9} \\approx 5.16 \\times 10^{-8}$ N\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-055","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":55,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about gravitational potential energy are correct?\n(i) Only differences in potential energy between two points have a definite physical meaning.\n(ii) Changing the arbitrary additive constant in the potential energy changes the gravitational force.\n(iii) The expression $mgh$ is an approximation to the potential energy difference, valid when $h$ is small compared with the earth's radius.\n(iv) With zero at infinity, the potential energy of a body near the earth is positive.\n(v) The potential energy of a system of particles is the sum of the potential energies of all possible pairs.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: only differences are measurable, so the zero can be chosen freely.\n(ii) Incorrect: the force does not depend on the constant.\n(iii) Correct: $mgh$ comes from approximating $-\\frac{GMm}{r}$ for $h \\ll R_E$.\n(iv) Incorrect: it is negative.\n(v) Correct: this is the superposition principle applied to pairs.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-056","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":56,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"A satellite revolves in a circular orbit at a height equal to the radius of the earth above its surface. Take $g = 10$ m/s$^2$ at the surface and $R_E = 6400$ km. What is the orbital speed of the satellite?","image":"","options":["$5.66$ km/s","$8.0$ km/s","$4.0$ km/s","$11.3$ km/s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $h = R_E$, so the orbit radius is $r = 2R_E$.\nFormula: $v^2 = \\frac{GM_E}{r}$ with $GM_E = gR_E^2$, so $v = \\sqrt{\\frac{gR_E}{2}}$.\nSubstituting: $v = \\sqrt{\\frac{10 \\times 6.4\\times10^6}{2}} = \\sqrt{3.2\\times10^7}$ m/s\nSo $v \\approx 5.66$ km/s.\nNote: $8.0$ km/s is the speed of a satellite orbiting close to the surface ($h \\approx 0$).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-057","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":57,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the following statements about acceleration due to gravity for a uniform-density earth.\nStatement I: For a small distance x (x much less than $R_E$), the decrease in g on going up a height x above the surface is about twice the decrease on going down a depth x below the surface.\nStatement II: g is maximum at the centre of the earth because a point at the centre is closest to all of the earth's mass.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I: Correct. Above the surface, $g(h) \\approx g\\left(1 - \\frac{2h}{R_E}\\right)$, so the decrease is $\\frac{2gx}{R_E}$. Below the surface, $g(d) = g\\left(1 - \\frac{d}{R_E}\\right)$, so the decrease is $\\frac{gx}{R_E}$. The first is twice the second.\nStatement II: Incorrect. At $d = R_E$, $g(d) = 0$. At the centre the pulls of all the surrounding mass cancel, so g is zero, not maximum.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-058","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":58,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"A planet moves around the Sun (treated as fixed) in an elliptical orbit. How many of the following quantities of the planet remain constant throughout its motion?\n(i) Angular momentum about the Sun\n(ii) Total mechanical energy\n(iii) Linear momentum\n(iv) Kinetic energy\n(v) Areal velocity","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Constant: gravity is a central force, so it exerts no torque about the Sun.\n(ii) Constant: gravity is conservative, so total mechanical energy is conserved.\n(iii) Not constant: the direction of velocity keeps changing.\n(iv) Not constant: the speed is greater at perihelion than at aphelion.\n(v) Constant: this is Kepler's second law, which follows from (i).\nSo 3 quantities stay constant.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-059","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":59,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"A rocket is fired vertically upward from the Earth's surface with a speed of 4 km/s. Take g = 10 m/s$^2$ at the surface and $R_E$ = 6400 km. Neglect air resistance and the Earth's rotation, and take into account that g decreases with height. What is the maximum height reached by the rocket above the surface?","image":"","options":["800 km","457 km","914 km","1067 km"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: $v = 4\\times10^3$ m/s, $g = 10$ m/s$^2$, $R_E = 6.4\\times10^6$ m.\nFormula (energy conservation): $\\frac{1}{2}mv^2 - \\frac{GMm}{R_E} = -\\frac{GMm}{R_E+h}$, with $GM = gR_E^2$. This gives $h = \\frac{R_E v^2}{2gR_E - v^2}$.\nSubstituting: $h = \\frac{6.4\\times10^6 \\times 1.6\\times10^7}{1.28\\times10^8 - 1.6\\times10^7} = 9.14\\times10^5$ m.\nSo $h \\approx 914$ km. The value 800 km comes from $v^2/2g$, which wrongly assumes g stays constant.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-060","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":60,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"Two uniform spheres, each of mass 100 kg and radius 0.10 m, have their centres 1.0 m apart on a horizontal table. Take $G = 6.67\\times10^{-11}$ N m$^2$ kg$^{-2}$ and zero potential at infinity. What is the gravitational potential at the midpoint of the line joining their centres?","image":"","options":["$-1.33\\times10^{-8}$ J/kg","Zero","$-2.67\\times10^{-8}$ J/kg","$-5.34\\times10^{-8}$ J/kg"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: $M = 100$ kg for each sphere. The midpoint is 0.5 m from each centre, which is outside both spheres.\nFormula: $V = -\\frac{GM}{r} - \\frac{GM}{r} = -\\frac{2GM}{0.5}$.\nSubstituting: $V = -4\\times6.67\\times10^{-11}\\times100$.\nSo $V \\approx -2.67\\times10^{-8}$ J/kg.\nThe field at the midpoint is zero (the pulls cancel), but the potentials add.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-061","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":61,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"Two uniform solid spheres, each of radius R, have masses M and 9M. Their centres are 8R apart, and both spheres are held fixed. A projectile is launched from the surface of the sphere of mass M directly towards the centre of the other sphere. What is the minimum launch speed for the projectile to reach the surface of the sphere of mass 9M?","image":"","options":["$\\sqrt{\\frac{2GM}{7R}}$","$\\sqrt{\\frac{3GM}{5R}}$","$\\sqrt{\\frac{GM}{R}}$","$\\sqrt{\\frac{4GM}{7R}}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Neutral point, at distance r from the centre of M: $\\frac{GM}{r^2} = \\frac{9GM}{(8R-r)^2}$, so $8R - r = 3r$ and $r = 2R$.\nThe projectile only needs to reach N; beyond N, the pull of 9M takes over.\nEnergy per unit mass at the launch surface: $\\frac{v^2}{2} - \\frac{GM}{R} - \\frac{9GM}{7R}$.\nEnergy per unit mass at N (speed zero): $-\\frac{GM}{2R} - \\frac{9GM}{6R} = -\\frac{2GM}{R}$.\nEquating: $\\frac{v^2}{2} = \\frac{GM}{R}\\left(\\frac{16}{7} - 2\\right) = \\frac{2GM}{7R}$, so $v = \\sqrt{\\frac{4GM}{7R}}$.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-062","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":62,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"A satellite of mass 500 kg moves in a circular orbit of radius $2R_E$ around the earth. How much energy must be supplied to shift it to a circular orbit of radius $3R_E$? Take $R_E = 6400$ km and g = 10 m/s$^2$ at the earth's surface.","image":"","options":["$2.67 \\times 10^{9}$ J","$5.33 \\times 10^{9}$ J","$8.00 \\times 10^{9}$ J","$1.78 \\times 10^{9}$ J"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: m = 500 kg, $r_1 = 2R_E$, $r_2 = 3R_E$, and $GM_E = gR_E^2$.\nFormula: in a circular orbit the total energy is $E = -\\frac{GM_Em}{2r}$. So the energy needed is $\\Delta E = \\frac{GM_Em}{2}\\left(\\frac{1}{r_1} - \\frac{1}{r_2}\\right)$.\nSubstituting: $\\Delta E = \\frac{gR_Em}{2}\\left(\\frac{1}{2} - \\frac{1}{3}\\right) = \\frac{gR_Em}{12} = \\frac{10 \\times 6.4\\times10^6 \\times 500}{12}$.\nSo $\\Delta E \\approx 2.67\\times10^{9}$ J.\nOption (2) uses only the change in PE and ignores the change in KE. Option (3) is the energy needed to escape from $2R_E$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-063","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":63,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"An ellipse has two foci. If the two foci are made to coincide at a single point, the ellipse becomes a","image":"","options":["parabola, with the semi-major axis becoming infinite","circle, with the semi-major axis becoming its radius","straight line, with the semi-major axis becoming zero","circle, with the semi-major axis becoming its diameter"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT treats the circle as a special case of the ellipse.\nWhen the foci $F_1$ and $F_2$ merge, every point on the curve is the same distance from that single point. The curve is therefore a circle.\nThe semi-major axis PO = AO is measured from the centre, so it becomes the radius.\nIt does not become the diameter, because the diameter is the full length PA.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-064","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":64,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"A body weighs 480 N on the surface of the earth. Assume the earth is a uniform sphere of radius 6400 km. What will the body weigh at a depth of 1600 km below the surface?","image":"","options":["360 N","240 N","270 N","202.5 N"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: W = 480 N, d = 1600 km, $R_E$ = 6400 km, so $d/R_E = 0.25$.\nFormula: weight is proportional to g, and $g(d) = g\\left(1 - \\frac{d}{R_E}\\right)$.\nSubstituting: $W(d) = 480 \\times (1 - 0.25) = 480 \\times 0.75$.\nSo W(d) = 360 N. The mass of the body itself is unchanged.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-065","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":65,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: For a small distance $x$ ($x \\ll R_E$), the fractional decrease in $g$ at a height $x$ above the earth's surface is twice the fractional decrease at a depth $x$ below the surface.\nStatement II: For an earth of uniform density, the acceleration due to gravity at the centre of the earth is zero.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I: at height $x$, $g(x) \\approx g(1 - 2x/R_E)$, so the fractional decrease is $2x/R_E$. At depth $x$, $g(x) = g(1 - x/R_E)$, so the fractional decrease is $x/R_E$. The first is twice the second, so Statement I is correct.\nStatement II: putting $d = R_E$ in $g(d) = g(1 - d/R_E)$ gives zero, so Statement II is correct.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-066","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":66,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"A body weighs 720 N on the surface of the Earth. What will it weigh at a height above the surface equal to the radius of the Earth?","image":"","options":["360 N","180 N","240 N","90 N"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: W = 720 N at the surface, h = $R_E$.\nFormula: $g(h) = \\frac{GM_E}{(R_E+h)^2}$, so $\\frac{W(h)}{W} = \\left(\\frac{R_E}{R_E+h}\\right)^2$.\nSubstituting: $\\frac{W(h)}{720} = \\left(\\frac{R_E}{2R_E}\\right)^2 = \\frac{1}{4}$.\nSo W(h) = 180 N. The option 360 N wrongly assumes g falls as $1/r$ instead of $1/r^2$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-067","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":67,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"According to NCERT, the escape speed from the surface of the Moon is about 2.3 km/s. Which of the following best explains why the Moon has no atmosphere?","image":"","options":["The Moon has no gravitational field at its surface","Gas molecules cannot be formed on the Moon's surface","Gas molecules on the Moon's surface can have speeds larger than the Moon's low escape speed and so escape its gravitational pull","The Sun's gravitational pull removes every gas molecule from the Moon"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Key fact: the escape speed is $\\sqrt{2gR}$, and both g and R are smaller for the Moon than for the Earth.\nSo the Moon's escape speed is only about 2.3 km/s, roughly five times smaller than the Earth's 11.2 km/s.\nGas molecules at the Moon's surface that move faster than 2.3 km/s escape, so an atmosphere cannot be held.\nOption (1) is wrong: the Moon does have surface gravity, only a weaker one.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-068","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":68,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"A point mass m is placed inside a thin, uniform hollow spherical shell of mass M and radius R, at a distance R/3 from its centre. What is the gravitational force exerted by the shell on the point mass?","image":"","options":["$\\frac{9GMm}{R^2}$","$\\frac{GMm}{R^2}$","Zero","$\\frac{GMm}{3R^2}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT result: a uniform hollow spherical shell exerts zero gravitational force on a point mass anywhere inside it.\nDifferent parts of the shell pull the mass in different directions, and these pulls cancel completely.\nThis holds at every interior point, not just at the centre. So the off-centre position does not matter.\n$\\frac{9GMm}{R^2}$ wrongly treats the shell's mass as if it were at the centre, a distance R/3 away.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-069","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":69,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"A planet moves around the Sun in an elliptical orbit. Its perihelion distance is $1.0 \\times 10^{11}$ m and its aphelion distance is $3.0 \\times 10^{11}$ m. Take $GM_{Sun} = 1.33 \\times 10^{20}\\ \\text{m}^3\\text{s}^{-2}$. The speed of the planet at perihelion is approximately:","image":"","options":["25.8 km/s","36.5 km/s","44.7 km/s","14.9 km/s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: $r_P = 1.0\\times10^{11}$ m, $r_A = 3.0\\times10^{11}$ m.\nAngular momentum: $v_P r_P = v_A r_A$, so $v_A = \\frac{v_P}{3}$.\nEnergy: $\\frac{1}{2}v_P^2 - \\frac{GM}{r_P} = \\frac{1}{2}v_A^2 - \\frac{GM}{r_A}$.\nCombining: $v_P^2 = \\frac{2GM r_A}{r_P(r_P + r_A)} = \\frac{2 \\times 1.33\\times10^{20} \\times 3\\times10^{11}}{10^{11} \\times 4\\times10^{11}} \\approx 2.0\\times10^9\\ \\text{m}^2\\text{s}^{-2}$.\nSo $v_P \\approx$ 44.7 km/s. 36.5 km/s is the circular-orbit speed at $r_P$, which is too small for an ellipse that reaches out to $r_A$.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-070","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":70,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"In the elliptical orbit of a planet around the Sun, the point on the orbit closest to the Sun is called the:","image":"","options":["aphelion","perihelion","focus","centre of the ellipse"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The point of the orbit nearest the Sun is the perihelion (P in Fig. 7.1a).\nThe farthest point is the aphelion (A).\nThe Sun sits at a focus of the ellipse, and the focus is not a point on the orbit.\nThe centre O is the midpoint of PA. It is also not on the orbit.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-071","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":71,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"A satellite of mass $600$ kg is in a circular orbit of radius $2R_E$ about the earth. How much energy must be supplied to shift it to a circular orbit of radius $3R_E$? Take $g = 10$ m/s$^2$ and $R_E = 6.4\\times10^6$ m.","image":"","options":["$3.2\\times10^9$ J","$6.4\\times10^9$ J","$1.6\\times10^9$ J","$9.6\\times10^9$ J"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $m = 600$ kg, $r_1 = 2R_E$, $r_2 = 3R_E$, $GM_E = gR_E^2$.\nFormula: $E = -\\frac{GM_Em}{2r}$, so $\\Delta E = \\frac{GM_Em}{2}\\left(\\frac{1}{r_1} - \\frac{1}{r_2}\\right)$.\nSubstituting: $\\Delta E = \\frac{gR_E^2m}{2}\\left(\\frac{1}{2R_E} - \\frac{1}{3R_E}\\right) = \\frac{mgR_E}{12} = \\frac{600\\times10\\times6.4\\times10^6}{12}$\nSo $\\Delta E = 3.2\\times10^9$ J.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-072","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":72,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"A planet moves in an elliptical orbit under the gravitational pull of the Sun. Assume the Sun is fixed. How many of the following quantities of the planet remain constant during its motion?\nLinear momentum, angular momentum about the Sun, total mechanical energy, kinetic energy, areal velocity","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Linear momentum is not constant. A net force (gravity) acts on the planet and keeps changing its velocity.\nAngular momentum about the Sun is constant, because gravity is a central force and exerts no torque about the Sun.\nTotal mechanical energy is constant, because gravity is conservative.\nKinetic energy is not constant. The speed is larger at perihelion than at aphelion.\nAreal velocity is constant, which is Kepler's second law.\nSo 3 quantities are constant.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-073","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":73,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"A student draws an ellipse by the string-and-pins method. A string of total length 20 cm has its ends pinned at two points $F_1$ and $F_2$, which are 12 cm apart. A pencil keeps the string taut while tracing the curve. What is the semi-major axis of the ellipse obtained?","image":"","options":["6 cm","8 cm","10 cm","20 cm"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: string length = 20 cm and focal separation = 12 cm.\nFor any point T on the ellipse, $TF_1 + TF_2$ equals the string length, so the sum is 20 cm.\nAt the end P of the major axis, this sum equals the full major axis $2a$. So $2a = 20$ cm.\nSo the semi-major axis $a$ = 10 cm. The value 8 cm is the semi-minor axis, not the semi-major axis.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-074","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":74,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"Two identical uniform spheres, each of mass 100 kg and radius 0.10 m, rest on a table with their centres 1.0 m apart.\nStatement I: At the midpoint of the line joining their centres, the gravitational field is zero but the gravitational potential is not zero.\nStatement II: A small object placed exactly at that midpoint is in stable equilibrium along the line joining the centres.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. The two equal pulls are opposite, so the field is zero. Potentials are scalars and add: $V = -2\\frac{G(100)}{0.5} \\approx -2.67\\times10^{-8}$ J/kg, which is not zero.\nStatement II is incorrect. If the object is shifted slightly toward one sphere, the pull of that nearer sphere becomes larger. The net force then pushes it further away from the midpoint, so the equilibrium is unstable.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-075","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":75,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"A body of mass m is near the earth (mass $M_E$, radius $R_E$, surface gravity g). Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(A) Escape speed from the surface\n(B) Escape speed from height h\n(C) Speed of a satellite orbiting close to the surface\n(D) Minimum energy to be given to the body at the surface so that it escapes\nList-II\n(I) $\\sqrt{gR_E}$\n(II) $\\frac{GM_Em}{R_E}$\n(III) $\\sqrt{2gR_E}$\n(IV) $\\sqrt{\\frac{2GM_E}{R_E+h}}$","image":"","options":["A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-I, B-IV, C-III, D-II","A-III, B-II, C-I, D-IV","A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) Setting $\\frac{1}{2}mv^2 = \\frac{GM_Em}{R_E}$ gives $v_e = \\sqrt{2gR_E}$, which is (III).\n(B) Replacing $R_E$ by $R_E + h$ gives $\\sqrt{\\frac{2GM_E}{R_E+h}}$, which is (IV).\n(C) For an orbit near the surface, $V^2 = \\frac{GM_E}{R_E} = gR_E$, so $V = \\sqrt{gR_E}$, which is (I).\n(D) The kinetic energy must cancel the negative potential energy $-\\frac{GM_Em}{R_E}$, so the energy needed is $\\frac{GM_Em}{R_E}$, which is (II).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-076","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":76,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"For an earth of uniform density, consider the following statements.\n(i) Below the surface, g decreases linearly with depth d.\n(ii) The magnitude of g is maximum at the surface of the earth.\n(iii) For every value of d between 0 and $R_E$, g at depth d is greater than g at height d above the surface.\n(iv) At the centre of the earth a body has zero weight, but its mass is unchanged.\nHow many of these statements are correct?","image":"","options":["3","2","4","1"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: $g(d) = g(1 - d/R_E)$ is linear in d.\n(ii) Correct: inside, g grows in proportion to r; outside, it falls as $1/r^2$. So g peaks at $r = R_E$.\n(iii) Incorrect: the comparison fails at large d. For $d = 0.9R_E$, depth gives 0.1g while height gives $g/(1.9)^2 \\approx 0.28g$.\n(iv) Correct: g = 0 at the centre, so weight is zero, but mass is an intrinsic property and does not change.\nSo three statements are correct.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-077","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":77,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"Treating the earth as a sphere of uniform density, what is the acceleration due to gravity at the centre of the earth?","image":"","options":["Zero","g, the same as at the surface","g/2","Infinitely large, because the distance from the centre is zero"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"At depth d, $g(d) = g\\left(1 - \\frac{d}{R_E}\\right)$.\nAt the centre, $d = R_E$, so $g(d) = g(1 - 1) = 0$.\nPhysically, every shell of the earth lies outside the point, and a uniform shell exerts no net force on a point inside it.\nThe value is not infinite: $\\frac{GM}{r^2}$ applies only with the mass enclosed within r, and that mass vanishes as $r^3$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-078","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":78,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"Four point masses of 10 kg each are placed at the corners of a square of side $\\sqrt{2}$ m. Taking $G = 6.67\\times10^{-11}$ N m$^2$ kg$^{-2}$ and zero potential at infinity, what is the gravitational potential at the centre of the square?","image":"","options":["$-1.33\\times10^{-9}$ J/kg","$-2.67\\times10^{-9}$ J/kg","$-1.89\\times10^{-9}$ J/kg","Zero, because the field at the centre is zero"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: $m = 10$ kg, $l = \\sqrt2$ m. The distance from the centre to each corner is $\\frac{l}{\\sqrt2} = 1$ m.\nFormula: potential is a scalar, so $V = -4\\frac{Gm}{r}$.\nSubstituting: $V = -4\\times6.67\\times10^{-11}\\times10/1$.\nSo $V \\approx -2.67\\times10^{-9}$ J/kg.\nThe field at the centre is zero by symmetry, but the potential is not, since contributions add as scalars.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-079","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":79,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) The gravitational force of a uniform hollow shell on a point mass is zero at every point inside the shell, not only at its centre.\n(ii) For a point outside a uniform shell, the force depends on the shell's radius even when the centre-to-point distance is fixed.\n(iii) Enclosing a mass inside a thick hollow lead sphere shields it from the Earth's gravitational pull.\n(iv) For a point inside a uniform Earth at distance r from the centre, only the mass within radius r contributes to the force.\n(v) The gravitational force between any two finite rigid bodies always acts along the line joining their centres of mass.","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: the zero-force result holds everywhere inside the shell.\n(ii) Incorrect: the shell acts as a point mass at its centre, so its radius does not matter.\n(iii) Incorrect: gravitational shielding is not possible.\n(iv) Correct: the outer shells exert zero force, and the inner sphere acts as if mass $M_r$ were at the centre.\n(v) Incorrect: this is true only for special shapes such as spherically symmetric bodies.\nSo 2 statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-080","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":80,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"The Moon revolves around the Earth in a nearly circular orbit of radius $3.84 \\times 10^8$ m with a period of 27.3 days. What is the Moon's centripetal acceleration?","image":"","options":["$2.72 \\times 10^{-3}$ m/s$^2$","$1.36 \\times 10^{-3}$ m/s$^2$","$5.44 \\times 10^{-3}$ m/s$^2$","$9.8$ m/s$^2$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $R_m = 3.84 \\times 10^8$ m, $T = 27.3 \\times 86400$ s $= 2.359 \\times 10^6$ s\nFormula: $a_m = \\frac{V^2}{R_m} = \\frac{4\\pi^2 R_m}{T^2}$\nSubstituting: $a_m = \\frac{4\\pi^2 \\times 3.84 \\times 10^8}{(2.359 \\times 10^6)^2}$\nSo $a_m \\approx 2.72 \\times 10^{-3}$ m/s$^2$, which is about $\\frac{g}{3600}$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-081","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":81,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The total mechanical energy of an earth satellite, taking potential energy at infinity as zero, is always negative.\nReason (R): If the total energy of an object in the earth's gravitational field were zero or positive, the object would escape to infinity.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. For a circular orbit E = $-\\frac{GMm}{2r}$. NCERT notes that the energy is negative for elliptical orbits as well.\nR is true. Zero or positive total energy means the object can reach infinity.\nA satellite stays at a finite distance, so its total energy cannot be zero or positive. R therefore correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-082","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":82,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about the acceleration due to gravity at the earth's surface is correct?","image":"","options":["It is larger for a heavier falling body","Its value increases as we go up from the surface","It is given by $g = \\frac{GM_E}{R_E^2}$, where $M_E$ is the mass of the earth","For a fixed mass of the earth, it is directly proportional to the earth's radius"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Incorrect: from F = mg with $F = \\frac{GM_Em}{R_E^2}$, the body's mass m cancels.\n(2) Incorrect: $g(h) = \\frac{GM_E}{(R_E+h)^2}$ decreases with height.\n(3) Correct: this is NCERT Eq. 7.12.\n(4) Incorrect: for fixed $M_E$, g varies as $\\frac{1}{R_E^2}$.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-083","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":83,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"Take the earth to be a uniform sphere of radius 6400 km with g = 9.8 m/s$^2$ at its surface. What is the acceleration due to gravity at the bottom of a mine 640 km deep?","image":"","options":["8.82 m/s$^2$","7.84 m/s$^2$","7.94 m/s$^2$","8.10 m/s$^2$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: g = 9.8 m/s$^2$, d = 640 km, $R_E$ = 6400 km.\nFormula: $g(d) = g\\left(1 - \\frac{d}{R_E}\\right)$.\nSubstituting: $g(d) = 9.8 \\times (1 - 0.1) = 9.8 \\times 0.9$.\nSo g(d) = 8.82 m/s$^2$.\nThe value 7.84 m/s$^2$ wrongly uses the height factor $(1 - 2d/R_E)$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-084","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":84,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"The escape speed of a body from the surface of the earth does NOT depend on which of the following?","image":"","options":["Mass of the body being projected","Mass of the earth","Radius of the earth","Acceleration due to gravity at the earth's surface"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Energy conservation gives $\\frac{1}{2}mv_e^2 = \\frac{GM_Em}{R_E}$.\nThe mass m of the body appears on both sides and cancels, so $v_e = \\sqrt{\\frac{2GM_E}{R_E}} = \\sqrt{2gR_E}$.\nThe result therefore depends on $M_E$, $R_E$ and g, but not on the projectile's mass.\nA heavier body needs more energy to escape, but it needs the same speed.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-085","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":85,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): A particle placed anywhere inside a uniform hollow spherical shell experiences no net gravitational force due to the shell.\nReason (R): A uniform hollow spherical shell shields a particle inside it from the gravitational forces of bodies placed outside the shell.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true. The different parts of the shell pull the inside particle in different directions, and these forces cancel completely. So the net force due to the shell is zero.\nR is false. Unlike a metallic shell, which shields electrical forces, the gravitational shell does not stop outside bodies from pulling the particle inside it.\nNCERT states that gravitational shielding is not possible. A particle inside the shell still feels the forces of external masses.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-086","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":86,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"The popular remark \"Cavendish weighed the earth\" refers to which of the following?","image":"","options":["By measuring $G$ in the laboratory, he made it possible to calculate the earth's mass from $M_E = \\frac{gR_E^2}{G}$, since $g$ and $R_E$ were already known","He balanced the earth against large lead spheres on a very sensitive beam balance","His torsion balance directly measured the gravitational force that the earth exerts on the small lead spheres","He measured $g$ very accurately, and this alone was enough to give the earth's mass without knowing $G$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"On the earth's surface, $g = \\frac{GM_E}{R_E^2}$, so $M_E = \\frac{gR_E^2}{G}$.\nThe values of $g$ and $R_E$ were already known. The missing quantity was $G$.\nCavendish's torsion-balance experiment (1798) gave the first measured value of $G$, so the earth's mass could then be calculated.\nOption (3) fails because his apparatus measured the attraction between the big and small lead spheres, not the earth's pull.\nOption (4) fails because $g$ alone cannot give $M_E$ without $G$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-087","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":87,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"Three point masses, each 2 kg, are placed at the vertices A, B and C of an equilateral triangle. A 4 kg mass is placed at the centroid G, which is 1 m from each vertex. The mass at A is now doubled to 4 kg. What is the magnitude of the net gravitational force on the mass at G? ($G = 6.67 \\times 10^{-11}$ N m$^2$/kg$^2$)","image":"","options":["$5.34 \\times 10^{-10}$ N","$1.07 \\times 10^{-9}$ N","$2.67 \\times 10^{-10}$ N","Zero"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Before the change, the three equal forces are at 120° to each other. By symmetry their sum is zero.\nAfter the change, write the 4 kg at A as 2 kg + 2 kg. The original three 2 kg masses still give zero.\nThe extra 2 kg at A gives the only unbalanced force, directed along GA.\nFormula: $F = \\frac{G(4)(2)}{1^2} = 8G$\nSubstituting: $8 \\times 6.67 \\times 10^{-11} = 5.34 \\times 10^{-10}$ N\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-088","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":88,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["The gravitational force exerted by a uniform hollow spherical shell on a point mass inside it is zero.","A uniform hollow spherical shell shields a particle inside it from the gravitational forces of bodies placed outside the shell.","A uniform spherical shell attracts an external point mass as if the whole mass of the shell were concentrated at its centre.","The gravitational force between two finite rigid bodies need not act along the line joining their centres of mass."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(1) Correct. The pulls from different parts of the shell cancel completely for a point inside it.\n(2) Incorrect. The shell exerts no force of its own on the particle inside, but it does not block forces from outside bodies. Gravitational shielding is not possible, unlike electrical shielding by a metallic shell.\n(3) Correct. This is the shell theorem for an external point.\n(4) Correct. This is stated in NCERT's Points to Ponder.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-089","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":89,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"A body is released from rest at a height equal to the earth's radius above the earth's surface. Neglect air resistance. With what speed does it hit the surface? Take $g = 10\\ m/s^2$ at the surface and $R_E = 6.4\\times10^{6}$ m.","image":"","options":["8.0 km/s","11.3 km/s","5.7 km/s","4.0 km/s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: the body starts at distance $2R_E$ from the centre and lands at distance $R_E$.\nFormula: $\\frac{1}{2}v^2 = GM_E\\left(\\frac{1}{R_E} - \\frac{1}{2R_E}\\right) = \\frac{GM_E}{2R_E}$, so $v = \\sqrt{\\frac{GM_E}{R_E}} = \\sqrt{gR_E}$.\nSubstituting: $v = \\sqrt{10\\times6.4\\times10^{6}} = 8000$ m/s.\nSo v = 8.0 km/s.\nThe option 11.3 km/s comes from $\\sqrt{2gh}$. That formula assumes g stays constant over a height of $R_E$, which is not valid.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-090","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":90,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"Treat the earth as a uniform sphere. Once G is known from Cavendish's experiment, the mean density of the earth can be found from g and $R_E$ alone. Taking $g = 9.8$ m s$^{-2}$, $R_E = 6.4\\times10^{6}$ m and $G = 6.67\\times10^{-11}$ N m$^2$ kg$^{-2}$, the mean density of the earth is closest to:","image":"","options":["$1.8\\times10^{3}$ kg m$^{-3}$","$2.7\\times10^{3}$ kg m$^{-3}$","$1.7\\times10^{4}$ kg m$^{-3}$","$5.5\\times10^{3}$ kg m$^{-3}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Given: g = 9.8 m s$^{-2}$, $R_E = 6.4\\times10^6$ m, $G = 6.67\\times10^{-11}$ N m$^2$ kg$^{-2}$.\nFormula: $g = \\frac{GM_E}{R_E^2}$ with $M_E = \\frac{4}{3}\\pi R_E^3\\rho$ gives $g = \\frac{4}{3}\\pi G\\rho R_E$, so $\\rho = \\frac{3g}{4\\pi G R_E}$.\nSubstituting: $\\rho = \\frac{3\\times9.8}{4\\pi\\times6.67\\times10^{-11}\\times6.4\\times10^6} = \\frac{29.4}{5.36\\times10^{-3}}$.\nSo $\\rho \\approx 5.5\\times10^3$ kg m$^{-3}$. Dropping the factor 3 would give $1.8\\times10^3$ kg m$^{-3}$, which is wrong.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-091","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":91,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"According to NCERT, the moon is the only natural satellite of the earth. Its orbit is nearly circular and its period of revolution is about 27.3 days. This period is roughly equal to","image":"","options":["the time taken by the earth to go once around the sun","the period of rotation of the moon about its own axis","the period of rotation of the earth about its own axis","the period of a satellite orbiting close to the earth's surface"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT notes that the moon's period of revolution (about 27.3 days) is roughly equal to its period of rotation about its own axis. \nThe earth's own rotation takes 1 day. \nA near-surface satellite takes only about 85 min. \nThe earth's revolution around the sun takes about 365 days. \nSo only option (2) matches. \nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-092","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":92,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"A satellite orbiting close to the earth's surface has a period of about 85 minutes. Another satellite moves in a circular orbit at a height $h = 3R_E$ above the surface, where $R_E$ is the radius of the earth. What is the period of the second satellite?","image":"","options":["340 min","680 min","1360 min","170 min"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: $T_0 = 85$ min for an orbit of radius $R_E$. The second orbit has radius $R_E + 3R_E = 4R_E$.\nFormula: by Kepler's third law, $T^2 \\propto (R_E + h)^3$, so $T = T_0\\left(\\frac{r}{R_E}\\right)^{3/2}$.\nSubstituting: $T = 85 \\times 4^{3/2} = 85 \\times 8$.\nSo $T = 680$ min. Using $h$ instead of the orbital radius would give the wrong value of 442 min.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-093","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":93,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"Four particles, each of mass 2 kg, are placed at the corners of a square of side 1.0 m. Take $G = 6.67\\times10^{-11}$ N m$^2$ kg$^{-2}$ and zero potential energy at infinite separation. What is the total gravitational potential energy of the system?","image":"","options":["$-1.07\\times10^{-9}$ J","$-1.44\\times10^{-9}$ J","$-1.82\\times10^{-9}$ J","$-0.72\\times10^{-9}$ J"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: $m = 2$ kg, $l = 1$ m.\nPairs: 4 side pairs at distance $l$ and 2 diagonal pairs at distance $\\sqrt2\\,l$.\nFormula: $U = -\\frac{Gm^2}{l}\\left(4 + \\frac{2}{\\sqrt2}\\right) = -(4+\\sqrt2)\\frac{Gm^2}{l} = -5.41\\frac{Gm^2}{l}$.\nSubstituting: $U = -5.41\\times6.67\\times10^{-11}\\times4$.\nSo $U \\approx -1.44\\times10^{-9}$ J. Option (1) ignores the diagonal pairs.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-094","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":94,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"On a planet the acceleration due to gravity at the surface is $5\\ m/s^2$ and the radius is $3.2 \\times 10^{6}$ m. What is the escape speed from its surface?","image":"","options":["5.66 km/s","4.00 km/s","8.00 km/s","2.83 km/s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $g = 5\\ m/s^2$ and $R = 3.2\\times10^{6}$ m.\nFormula: $v_e = \\sqrt{2gR}$.\nSubstituting: $v_e = \\sqrt{2\\times5\\times3.2\\times10^{6}} = \\sqrt{3.2\\times10^{7}}$ m/s $\\approx 5657$ m/s.\nSo $v_e \\approx 5.66$ km/s.\nThe option 4.00 km/s is $\\sqrt{gR}$, which is the speed of a satellite orbiting close to the surface, not the escape speed.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-095","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":95,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"A satellite of mass m moves in a circular orbit of radius r around the earth (mass M). Take potential energy at infinity as zero. Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(A) Kinetic energy of the satellite\n(B) Potential energy of the satellite\n(C) Total mechanical energy of the satellite\n(D) Magnitude of change in potential energy when the satellite is taken from r to infinity\nList-II\n(i) $-\\frac{GMm}{2r}$\n(ii) $\\frac{GMm}{2r}$\n(iii) $-\\frac{GMm}{r}$\n(iv) $\\frac{GMm}{r}$","image":"","options":["A-(ii), B-(iii), C-(i), D-(iv)","A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(ii), B-(i), C-(iii), D-(iv)","A-(i), B-(iii), C-(ii), D-(iv)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) KE = $\\frac{1}{2}mv^2$ with $v^2 = \\frac{GM}{r}$, giving $\\frac{GMm}{2r}$ → (ii).\n(B) PE = $-\\frac{GMm}{r}$ → (iii).\n(C) E = KE + PE = $-\\frac{GMm}{2r}$ → (i).\n(D) PE changes from $-\\frac{GMm}{r}$ to 0, a change of magnitude $\\frac{GMm}{r}$ → (iv).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-096","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":96,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The relation $g(h) \\approx g\\left(1 - \\frac{2h}{R_E}\\right)$ cannot be used to find g at a height equal to the earth's radius.\nReason (R): This relation is obtained by a binomial expansion that is valid only when $h \\ll R_E$.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: putting h = $R_E$ into the formula gives g(1 − 2) = −g, which is absurd. The actual value is $\\frac{g}{4}$.\nR is true: NCERT expands $(1 + h/R_E)^{-2}$ binomially and keeps only the first-order term. This is valid only for $h/R_E \\ll 1$.\nThe failure of the formula at h = $R_E$ is exactly because this condition is violated, so R explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-097","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":97,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about Newton's law of gravitation are correct?\n(i) The forces two particles exert on each other are equal in magnitude and opposite in direction.\n(ii) The force between two particles changes if a third particle is brought close to them.\n(iii) The force between two point masses acts along the line joining them.\n(iv) The value of $G$ depends on the masses of the interacting bodies.\n(v) The gravitational force between two masses is always attractive.","image":"","options":["3","2","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: the two forces form a Newton's third law pair.\n(ii) Incorrect: by superposition, each pair interacts independently of other masses.\n(iii) Correct: gravity between point masses is a central force.\n(iv) Incorrect: $G$ is a universal constant, $6.67 \\times 10^{-11}$ N m$^2$/kg$^2$.\n(v) Correct: gravity is always attractive.\nSo 3 statements are correct.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-098","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":98,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"A uniform-density earth of mass $M_E$ and radius $R_E$ is imagined as an inner sphere of radius $R_E/2$ surrounded by a shell. What fraction of $M_E$ is contained in the inner sphere?","image":"","options":["$\\frac{1}{8}$","$\\frac{1}{2}$","$\\frac{1}{4}$","$\\frac{1}{16}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: the inner sphere has radius $r = R_E/2$ and the density is uniform.\nFormula: mass is proportional to volume, so $\\frac{M_s}{M_E} = \\frac{r^3}{R_E^3}$.\nSubstituting: $\\frac{M_s}{M_E} = \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^3$.\nSo $M_s = \\frac{M_E}{8}$.\nThe option 1/2 would assume mass grows linearly with radius, which is wrong.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-099","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":99,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"Two identical heavy spheres are fixed with their centres a distance $d$ apart. A small particle is placed exactly at the midpoint of the line joining their centres.\nStatement I: The particle is in equilibrium at the midpoint.\nStatement II: If the particle is displaced slightly along the line joining the centres, gravity pulls it back to the midpoint, so this equilibrium is stable along that line.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct: the two equal and opposite pulls cancel, so the net force at the midpoint is zero.\nStatement II is incorrect: suppose the particle shifts towards one sphere. That sphere is now nearer, so its pull ($\\propto \\frac{1}{r^2}$) becomes larger than the other's.\nThe net force therefore points away from the midpoint, increasing the displacement. Along the line, the equilibrium is unstable.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-100","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":100,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"Take the gravitational potential energy to be zero at infinity and neglect air resistance. How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) A body projected from the earth's surface with exactly the escape speed has zero total mechanical energy.\n(ii) The escape speed from the moon is about 2.3 km/s.\n(iii) If a planet's mass is doubled while its radius stays the same, its escape speed doubles.\n(iv) A heavier body needs a larger escape speed than a lighter one from the same planet.\n(v) The escape speed from a point above the earth's surface is less than 11.2 km/s.","image":"","options":["3","2","4","1"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: at exactly the escape speed, the kinetic energy equals $\\frac{GMm}{R}$, so KE + PE = 0.\n(ii) Correct: NCERT gives about 2.3 km/s for the moon.\n(iii) Incorrect: $v_e \\propto \\sqrt{M}$, so doubling the mass increases $v_e$ by a factor of $\\sqrt{2}$, not 2.\n(iv) Incorrect: the mass of the projectile cancels in the energy equation, so $v_e$ does not depend on it.\n(v) Correct: $v = \\sqrt{\\frac{2GM_E}{R_E+h}}$ decreases as h increases, so it is below 11.2 km/s above the surface.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-101","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":101,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"A planet moves in an elliptical orbit of eccentricity e around the Sun. The minor axis of the orbit meets the ellipse at points B and C.\nStatement I: The planet has the same speed at B and at C.\nStatement II: The ratio of the planet's speed at perihelion to its speed at aphelion is $\\frac{1+e}{1-e}$.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I is true. By symmetry, B and C are at the same distance from the Sun at focus S (each distance equals a). The angle between the velocity and the radius vector is also the same at both points. With equal angular momentum and equal geometry, the speeds are equal. Energy conservation gives the same result.\nStatement II is true. $r_P = a(1-e)$ and $r_A = a(1+e)$. Since $v_P r_P = v_A r_A$, we get $\\frac{v_P}{v_A} = \\frac{r_A}{r_P} = \\frac{1+e}{1-e}$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-102","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":102,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"Four identical point masses of 2 kg each are placed at the corners of a square of side 1 m. What is the magnitude of the net gravitational force on any one of the masses due to the other three? ($G = 6.67 \\times 10^{-11}$ N m$^2$/kg$^2$)","image":"","options":["$5.11 \\times 10^{-10}$ N","$3.77 \\times 10^{-10}$ N","$6.67 \\times 10^{-10}$ N","$1.33 \\times 10^{-10}$ N"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: m = 2 kg, a = 1 m, so $\\frac{Gm^2}{a^2} = 4G$.\nThe two adjacent masses each give $4G$, at right angles. Their resultant is $4\\sqrt{2}G$, along the diagonal.\nThe diagonal mass is at distance $\\sqrt{2}$ m. It gives $\\frac{4G}{2} = 2G$, along the same diagonal.\nSubstituting: $F = 4G(\\sqrt{2} + 0.5) = 4 \\times 6.67 \\times 10^{-11} \\times 1.914$\nSo $F \\approx 5.11 \\times 10^{-10}$ N. The value $6.67 \\times 10^{-10}$ N wrongly adds the magnitudes as scalars.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-103","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":103,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): A point mass at depth d inside a uniform earth is attracted only by the sphere of radius $(R_E - d)$ beneath it.\nReason (R): A uniform spherical shell exerts zero net gravitational force on a point mass placed anywhere inside it.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The earth can be split into an inner sphere of radius $(R_E - d)$ and an outer shell of thickness d.\nThe point mass lies inside the outer shell, so by R the shell exerts no net force on it.\nOnly the inner sphere attracts the mass, acting as if all its mass were at the centre. So A is true.\nR is true and is exactly the reason A holds.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-104","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":104,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"The escape speed from the Earth's surface is 11.2 km/s. A planet has twice the radius of the Earth and half the Earth's mean density. What is the escape speed from the surface of this planet?","image":"","options":["15.8 km/s","11.2 km/s","22.4 km/s","7.9 km/s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $R_p=2R_E$, $\\rho_p=\\rho_E/2$ and $v_E=11.2$ km/s.\nFormula: $v_e=\\sqrt{\\frac{2GM}{R}}$. Substituting $M=\\frac{4}{3}\\pi R^3\\rho$ gives $v_e=R\\sqrt{\\frac{8\\pi G\\rho}{3}}$, so $v_e\\propto R\\sqrt{\\rho}$.\nSubstituting: $\\frac{v_p}{v_E}=2\\times\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{2}}=\\sqrt{2}$.\nSo $v_p=11.2\\times1.414\\approx15.8$ km/s.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-105","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":105,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: For a satellite in a circular orbit around the earth, the orbital speed does not depend on the mass of the satellite.\nStatement II: A satellite placed in a higher circular orbit moves with a greater orbital speed than one in a lower orbit.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Equating the centripetal force $\\frac{mv^2}{R_E+h}$ to the gravitational force $\\frac{GmM_E}{(R_E+h)^2}$ gives $v^2=\\frac{GM_E}{R_E+h}$. \nStatement I is correct: m cancels, so v does not depend on the satellite's mass. \nStatement II is incorrect: v varies as $(R_E+h)^{-1/2}$, so v decreases as h increases. \nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-106","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":106,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The centripetal acceleration of the Moon in its orbit is much smaller than the acceleration due to gravity at the Earth's surface.\nReason (R): The Earth's gravitational force falls off as the inverse square of the distance from the Earth's centre, and the Moon is about 60 Earth radii away.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: the Moon's acceleration is about $2.7 \\times 10^{-3}$ m/s$^2$, while $g \\approx 9.8$ m/s$^2$.\nR is true: gravity varies as $\\frac{1}{r^2}$, and $R_m \\approx 60R_E$.\nR explains A: $\\frac{a_m}{g} = \\left(\\frac{R_E}{R_m}\\right)^2 \\approx \\frac{1}{3600}$. This is Newton's famous Moon test.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-107","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":107,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"The total mechanical energy of a satellite in a circular orbit around the earth is $-5.0 \\times 10^{9}$ J (potential energy at infinity taken as zero). What is its kinetic energy?","image":"","options":["$5.0 \\times 10^{9}$ J","$2.5 \\times 10^{9}$ J","$1.0 \\times 10^{10}$ J","$-5.0 \\times 10^{9}$ J"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: E = $-5.0 \\times 10^{9}$ J.\nFormula: for a circular orbit, KE = $\\frac{GMm}{2r}$ and E = $-\\frac{GMm}{2r}$, so KE = −E.\nSubstituting: KE = $-(-5.0 \\times 10^{9})$.\nSo KE = $5.0 \\times 10^{9}$ J. Kinetic energy can never be negative, which rules out option (4).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-108","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":108,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"A body weighs 600 N on the surface of the Earth. Take the radius of the Earth as 6400 km and use the small-distance approximations for g. What is the difference between its weight at a depth of 32 km and its weight at a height of 32 km above the surface?","image":"","options":["3 N","6 N","1.5 N","0 N"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: W = 600 N, h = d = 32 km, $R_E$ = 6400 km.\nFormulas: $W_h = W(1-\\frac{2h}{R_E})$ and $W_d = W(1-\\frac{d}{R_E})$.\nSubstituting: $W_h = 600(1-0.01) = 594$ N and $W_d = 600(1-0.005) = 597$ N.\nSo the difference = 597 − 594 = 3 N. The body weighs more at the depth.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-109","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":109,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about the gravitational force is correct?","image":"","options":["The force between two point masses acts along the line joining them and is always attractive.","The force between any two finite rigid bodies always acts along the line joining their centres of mass.","The force between two point masses changes when a third mass is placed near them.","The force exerted by a heavier mass on a lighter one is larger than the force exerted by the lighter mass on the heavier one."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: for point masses the force is central, meaning it acts along the line joining them, and it is attractive.\n(2) Incorrect: NCERT notes that for finite rigid bodies the force is not necessarily along the line joining their centres of mass. It is central only for spherically symmetric bodies acting on external particles.\n(3) Incorrect: by superposition, each pair's force is unaffected by other masses.\n(4) Incorrect: the two forces form a Newton's third law pair, so they are equal in magnitude.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-110","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":110,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II for the earth (mass $M_E$, radius $R_E$, surface gravity $g$):\nList-I\n(a) Orbital speed of a satellite close to the surface\n(b) Period of a satellite close to the surface\n(c) Escape speed from the surface\n(d) Total energy of a satellite of mass $m$ in a circular orbit of radius $r$\nList-II\n(i) $2\\pi\\sqrt{\\frac{R_E}{g}}$\n(ii) $-\\frac{GM_Em}{2r}$\n(iii) $\\sqrt{gR_E}$\n(iv) $\\sqrt{2gR_E}$","image":"","options":["(a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(i), (c)-(iii), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(i)","(a)-(i), (b)-(iii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) $V^2 = \\frac{GM_E}{R_E} = gR_E$, so (a) matches (iii).\n(b) $T_0 = \\frac{2\\pi R_E}{V} = 2\\pi\\sqrt{\\frac{R_E}{g}}$, so (b) matches (i).\n(c) Setting the total energy to zero gives $v_e = \\sqrt{2gR_E}$, so (c) matches (iv).\n(d) KE $= \\frac{GM_Em}{2r}$ and PE $= -\\frac{GM_Em}{r}$, so $E = -\\frac{GM_Em}{2r}$, matching (ii).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-111","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":111,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"Two thin uniform spherical shells are concentric. The inner shell has mass 100 kg and radius 0.5 m. The outer shell has mass 300 kg and radius 2 m. A 1 kg point mass is placed 1 m from the common centre. Find the net gravitational force on it. (Take $G = 6.67\\times10^{-11}$ N m$^2$/kg$^2$.)","image":"","options":["$6.67\\times10^{-9}$ N","$26.7\\times10^{-9}$ N","$20.0\\times10^{-9}$ N","Zero"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: r = 1 m. The point lies outside the inner shell (0.5 m) but inside the outer shell (2 m).\nOuter shell: the point is inside it, so its force is zero.\nInner shell: the point is outside it, so $F = \\frac{GM_1m}{r^2}$ with $M_1$ = 100 kg.\nSubstituting: $F = \\frac{6.67\\times10^{-11}\\times100\\times1}{1^2}$.\nSo F = $6.67\\times10^{-9}$ N.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-112","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":112,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"A rocket is fired vertically upward from the earth's surface with a speed of 4 km/s. Neglect air resistance and the earth's rotation. How high above the surface does it rise? Take $G = 6.67\\times10^{-11}\\ N\\,m^2/kg^2$, $M_E = 6.0\\times10^{24}$ kg and $R_E = 6.4\\times10^{6}$ m.","image":"","options":["About 939 km","About 800 km","About 7339 km","About 470 km"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: v = 4000 m/s and $GM_E = 4.002\\times10^{14}\\ m^3/s^2$.\nFormula (energy conservation): $\\frac{1}{2}v^2 - \\frac{GM_E}{R_E} = -\\frac{GM_E}{R_E+h}$.\nSubstituting: $\\frac{GM_E}{R_E} = 6.253\\times10^{7}$ and $\\frac{1}{2}v^2 = 8.0\\times10^{6}$. So $\\frac{GM_E}{R_E+h} = 5.453\\times10^{7}$, which gives $R_E + h = 7.339\\times10^{6}$ m.\nSo $h \\approx 9.39\\times10^{5}$ m, or about 939 km.\nThe option 800 km comes from $\\frac{v^2}{2g}$. That formula ignores the fall in g with height, so it underestimates h.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-113","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":113,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"Treat the earth as a uniform sphere of radius R, with surface gravity g. Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I (location)\n(A) On the surface\n(B) At the centre\n(C) At depth R/2\n(D) At height R above the surface\nList-II (acceleration due to gravity)\n(i) 0\n(ii) g/4\n(iii) g\n(iv) g/2","image":"","options":["A-(iii), B-(i), C-(iv), D-(ii)","A-(iii), B-(i), C-(ii), D-(iv)","A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(i), B-(iii), C-(iv), D-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) At the surface the value is g by definition, so A-(iii).\n(B) At the centre, $g(1 - R/R) = 0$, so B-(i).\n(C) At depth R/2, $g(1 - \\frac{1}{2}) = \\frac{g}{2}$, so C-(iv).\n(D) At height R the distance from the centre is 2R. The exact formula gives $\\frac{gR^2}{(2R)^2} = \\frac{g}{4}$, so D-(ii). The approximation $(1 - 2h/R)$ is not valid when h = R.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-114","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":114,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The moon has no atmosphere.\nReason (R): The escape speed from the moon's surface is only about 2.3 km/s, so gas molecules with speeds greater than this escape its gravitational pull.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT states that the moon has no atmosphere.\nR is true. On the moon both g and the radius are smaller than on the earth, so its escape speed is about 2.3 km/s. This is roughly five times smaller than the earth's 11.2 km/s.\nGas molecules on the moon that move faster than 2.3 km/s escape, so the moon cannot hold an atmosphere. R therefore correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-115","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":115,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"Match the height h above the Earth's surface in List-I with the acceleration due to gravity at that height in List-II (g = value at the surface, $R_E$ = radius of the Earth).\nList-I\n(P) $h = R_E$\n(Q) $h = 2R_E$\n(R) $h = 3R_E$\n(S) $h = (\\sqrt{2} - 1)R_E$\nList-II\n(i) $\\frac{g}{2}$\n(ii) $\\frac{g}{9}$\n(iii) $\\frac{g}{16}$\n(iv) $\\frac{g}{4}$","image":"","options":["P-(iv), Q-(ii), R-(iii), S-(i)","P-(i), Q-(iv), R-(ii), S-(iii)","P-(iv), Q-(iii), R-(ii), S-(i)","P-(ii), Q-(iv), R-(iii), S-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Use $g(h) = g\\left(\\frac{R_E}{R_E+h}\\right)^2$.\n(P) $R_E + h = 2R_E$, so g(h) = g/4, which is (iv).\n(Q) $R_E + h = 3R_E$, so g(h) = g/9, which is (ii).\n(R) $R_E + h = 4R_E$, so g(h) = g/16, which is (iii).\n(S) $R_E + h = \\sqrt{2}R_E$, so g(h) = g/2, which is (i).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-116","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":116,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"A satellite moves around the earth in an elliptical orbit of semi-major axis a. Which of the following statements is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["Its kinetic energy is greater at the point nearest to the earth than at the farthest point","Its potential energy is more negative at the nearest point than at the farthest point","Its total mechanical energy is $-\\frac{GMm}{2a}$ at every point of the orbit","Its speed is the same at all points because its total energy is constant"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: by the law of areas, the satellite moves fastest when nearest to the earth.\n(2) Correct: PE = $-\\frac{GMm}{r}$ is more negative when r is smaller.\n(3) Correct: the total energy stays constant at $-\\frac{GMm}{2a}$.\n(4) Incorrect: a constant total energy does not mean constant speed. KE and PE both vary from point to point while their sum stays fixed.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-117","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":117,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"For a point mass m inside a uniform-density earth at distance r from the centre, the gravitational force on m varies as","image":"","options":["$r$","$\\frac{1}{r^2}$","$\\frac{1}{r}$","$r^3$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Only the mass inside radius r attracts m: $M_r = M_E\\frac{r^3}{R_E^3}$.\nTherefore $F = \\frac{GmM_r}{r^2} = \\frac{GmM_E}{R_E^3}\\,r$, so F is proportional to r.\nThe inverse-square form, $1/r^2$, holds only outside the earth.\nThe option $r^3$ describes the enclosed mass, not the force.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-118","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":118,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"A point mass is held at a fixed distance d from the centre of a uniform thin spherical shell of mass M, where d is greater than the shell radius. The shell is slowly expanded to a larger radius, keeping its mass M, its centre fixed and its radius still less than d. The gravitational force on the point mass:","image":"","options":["increases","decreases","remains unchanged","becomes zero"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"For an external point, the shell behaves as if all its mass M were at its centre: $F = \\frac{GMm}{d^2}$.\nThis expression contains no shell radius. Only M and the centre-to-point distance d matter.\nSince M and d are both unchanged, the force stays the same.\nThe force would become zero only if the point ended up inside the shell, which does not happen here.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-119","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":119,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"A satellite revolves in a circular orbit very close to the earth's surface. Take $R_E = 6400$ km and g = 10 m/s$^2$. Its time period is approximately","image":"","options":["83.8 min","41.9 min","167.6 min","120.0 min"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $R_E = 6.4\\times10^{6}$ m, g = 10 m/s$^2$, and h is negligible. \nFormula: $T_0 = 2\\pi\\sqrt{R_E/g}$ \nSubstituting: $T_0 = 2\\pi\\sqrt{6.4\\times10^{6}/10} = 2\\pi\\times800 \\approx 5027$ s \nSo $T_0 \\approx 83.8$ min. This is close to the NCERT value of about 85 min, which uses g = 9.8 m/s$^2$. \nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-120","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":120,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"A satellite of mass 500 kg is in a circular orbit of radius $2R_E$ around the earth. How much energy is needed to shift it to a circular orbit of radius $4R_E$? Take $R_E = 6.4 \\times 10^{6}$ m and g = 10 m/s$^2$ at the earth's surface.","image":"","options":["$2.0 \\times 10^{9}$ J","$4.0 \\times 10^{9}$ J","$8.0 \\times 10^{9}$ J","$1.6 \\times 10^{10}$ J"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: m = 500 kg, $r_1 = 2R_E$, $r_2 = 4R_E$, $GM = gR_E^2$.\nFormula: $\\Delta E = E_2 - E_1 = \\frac{GMm}{2}\\left(\\frac{1}{r_1} - \\frac{1}{r_2}\\right)$.\nSubstituting: $\\Delta E = \\frac{gR_E^2 m}{2}\\left(\\frac{1}{2R_E} - \\frac{1}{4R_E}\\right) = \\frac{gR_E m}{8}$.\nThis gives $\\frac{10 \\times 6.4 \\times 10^{6} \\times 500}{8}$.\nSo $\\Delta E = 4.0 \\times 10^{9}$ J.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-121","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":121,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"A body weighs 800 N on the surface of the earth. What will it weigh at a height equal to the radius of the earth ($h = R_E$) above the surface?","image":"","options":["400 N","200 N","100 N","0 N"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: surface weight W = 800 N, h = $R_E$.\nFormula: $g(h) = \\frac{GM_E}{(R_E+h)^2}$, so $\\frac{W(h)}{W} = \\left(\\frac{R_E}{R_E+h}\\right)^2$.\nSubstituting: $\\left(\\frac{R_E}{2R_E}\\right)^2 = \\frac{1}{4}$, so W(h) = 800/4.\nSo the weight = 200 N. Taking 400 N wrongly assumes g falls as $\\frac{1}{r}$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-122","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":122,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"Ignore air resistance and the rotation of the Earth. How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) The escape speed from the Earth's surface does not depend on the mass of the projectile.\n(ii) The escape speed depends on the direction in which the projectile is launched.\n(iii) The escape speed from the Earth's surface can be written as $\\sqrt{2gR_E}$.\n(iv) The escape speed from a point at height h above the Earth's surface is greater than that from the surface.\n(v) The escape speed from the Moon's surface is about five times smaller than that from the Earth's surface.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: m cancels in $\\frac{1}{2}mv^2 = \\frac{GMm}{R}$.\n(ii) Incorrect: the condition comes from energy, which is a scalar, so the direction does not matter.\n(iii) Correct: using $g = GM_E/R_E^2$ in $v_e = \\sqrt{2GM_E/R_E}$ gives $\\sqrt{2gR_E}$.\n(iv) Incorrect: $v = \\sqrt{2GM_E/(R_E+h)}$ is smaller at a height.\n(v) Correct: 2.3 km/s versus 11.2 km/s.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-123","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":123,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"Four particles, each of mass $m$, sit at the corners of a square of side $l$. The minimum work an external agent must do to remove one particle to infinity, leaving the other three fixed, is:","image":"","options":["$(4+\\sqrt2)\\frac{Gm^2}{l}$","$\\left(1+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt2}\\right)\\frac{Gm^2}{l}$","$\\left(2+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt2}\\right)\\frac{Gm^2}{l}$","$\\left(3+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt2}\\right)\\frac{Gm^2}{l}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Removing one particle eliminates only the pair energies that involve it.\nThis particle has 2 neighbours at distance $l$ and 1 diagonal partner at distance $\\sqrt2\\,l$.\nIts interaction energy is $-\\frac{Gm^2}{l}\\left(2+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt2}\\right)$. At infinity this energy becomes zero.\nThe minimum work equals the rise in potential energy: $\\left(2+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt2}\\right)\\frac{Gm^2}{l} \\approx 2.71\\frac{Gm^2}{l}$.\nOption (1) is the magnitude of the energy needed to separate all four particles.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-124","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":124,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"In our solar system, the inter-planetary region has chunks of matter (much smaller in size compared to planets) called asteroids. They","image":"","options":["will not move around the sun since they have very small masses compared to sun.","will move in an irregular way because of their small masses and will drift away into outer space.","will move around the sun in closed orbits but not obey Kepler’s laws.","will move in orbits like planets and obey Kepler’s laws."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Key fact: Asteroids are bound mainly by the sun's inverse-square gravitational pull, just like planets.\nKepler's laws follow from an inverse-square central force. They do not depend on the mass of the orbiting body, because that mass cancels in $\\frac{GMm}{r^2}=\\frac{mv^2}{r}$.\nSo asteroids move in elliptical orbits and obey all three of Kepler's laws.\nA small mass does not stop a body from orbiting or make it drift away, so (1) and (2) are wrong. (3) is wrong because they do obey Kepler's laws.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Physics Class 11, Ch 8 (MCQ Q8.6)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-125","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":125,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"The acceleration due to gravity at a height of 32 km above Earth's surface is equal to that at a depth d below the surface. Take the radius of Earth as 6400 km and use the small-height approximation. The value of d is:","image":"","options":["16 km","32 km","64 km","128 km"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: h = 32 km and $R_E$ = 6400 km, so h is much smaller than $R_E$.\nFormula: $g(h) \\approx g(1-\\frac{2h}{R_E})$ and $g(d) = g(1-\\frac{d}{R_E})$.\nSetting g(h) = g(d) gives $\\frac{2h}{R_E} = \\frac{d}{R_E}$, so d = 2h.\nSubstituting: d = 2 × 32 = 64 km.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-126","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":126,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"Take the earth to be a uniform sphere of radius 6400 km. At what depth below the surface is the acceleration due to gravity equal to its value at a height of 3200 km above the surface? (Do not use the small-height approximation.)","image":"","options":["3555.6 km","6400 km","3200 km","2844.4 km"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $R_E = 6400$ km and $h = 3200$ km $= \\frac{R_E}{2}$.\nAt that height: $g(h) = \\frac{g}{(1 + h/R_E)^2} = \\frac{g}{(1.5)^2} = \\frac{4g}{9}$.\nAt depth: $g(d) = g\\left(1 - \\frac{d}{R_E}\\right)$. Setting this equal gives $1 - \\frac{d}{R_E} = \\frac{4}{9}$, so $d = \\frac{5R_E}{9}$.\nSubstituting: $d = \\frac{5}{9} \\times 6400 \\approx 3555.6$ km.\nUsing $\\frac{2h}{R_E}$ would give $d = 2h = 6400$ km. That is wrong because h is not small compared with $R_E$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-127","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":127,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: For a satellite in a circular orbit around the Earth, its kinetic energy equals half the magnitude of its gravitational potential energy (with potential energy taken as zero at infinity).\nStatement II: For a satellite in an elliptical orbit, both its kinetic and potential energies vary from point to point, but its total energy stays constant and negative.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I is true: $K=\\frac{GmM_E}{2r}$ and $U=-\\frac{GmM_E}{r}$, so $K=|U|/2$ and $E=-\\frac{GmM_E}{2r}$.\nStatement II is true: in an elliptic orbit, speed and distance both change, so K and U change. The total energy is conserved and remains negative, $E=-\\frac{GMm}{2a}$. If it were zero or positive, the satellite would escape.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-128","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":128,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) Copernicus proposed a model in which planets move in circles around a fixed central Sun.\n(ii) Ptolemy's model placed the Sun at the centre, with all planets revolving around it.\n(iii) Kepler extracted his three laws from data recorded by Tycho Brahe.\n(iv) Tycho Brahe's planetary observations were made with a powerful telescope.\n(v) Kepler's first law was a departure from the Copernican model, which allowed only circular orbits.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: the Copernican model had circular orbits around a fixed Sun.\n(ii) Incorrect: Ptolemy's model was geocentric, with the Earth at the centre.\n(iii) Correct: Kepler, Tycho Brahe's assistant, analysed Brahe's data.\n(iv) Incorrect: Brahe recorded his observations with the naked eye.\n(v) Correct: elliptical orbits were a deviation from the purely circular Copernican orbits.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-129","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":129,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): An earth satellite in a circular orbit at a greater height moves with a smaller orbital speed.\nReason (R): For a circular orbit of radius $(R_E + h)$, the orbital speed satisfies $V^2 = \\frac{GM_E}{R_E + h}$.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Equating the centripetal force $\\frac{mV^2}{R_E+h}$ to the gravitational force $\\frac{GM_Em}{(R_E+h)^2}$ gives $V^2 = \\frac{GM_E}{R_E+h}$, so R is true.\nThis relation shows that $V$ decreases as $h$ increases, so A is true.\nA follows directly from R, so R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-130","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":130,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"The escape speed on the earth's surface is 11.2 km/s. A body is projected from the surface with twice this speed. Ignoring the effects of the sun and other planets, what is its speed when it is very far from the earth?","image":"","options":["19.4 km/s","22.4 km/s","11.2 km/s","25.0 km/s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: the initial speed is $v_i = 2v_e$. The potential energy is zero at infinity.\nFormula: $\\frac{1}{2}mv_i^2 - \\frac{GM_Em}{R_E} = \\frac{1}{2}mv_f^2$, and $\\frac{GM_E}{R_E} = \\frac{1}{2}v_e^2$.\nSubstituting: $v_f^2 = v_i^2 - v_e^2 = 4v_e^2 - v_e^2 = 3v_e^2$.\nSo $v_f = \\sqrt{3}\\times11.2 \\approx 19.4$ km/s.\nThe option 11.2 km/s wrongly subtracts the speeds directly ($2v_e - v_e$) instead of subtracting their squares.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-131","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":131,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"Masses are placed at the vertices A, B, C of an equilateral triangle, and a mass $2m$ is placed at its centroid G, with GA = GB = GC = 1 m. Let $\\hat{j}$ be the unit vector from G towards A. Match each configuration in List-I with the net gravitational force on $2m$ in List-II.\nList-I\n(P) Mass $m$ at each of A, B and C\n(Q) Mass at A doubled to $2m$, mass $m$ at B and C\n(R) Mass at A removed, mass $m$ at B and C\n(S) Mass at A replaced by $3m$, mass $m$ at B and C\nList-II\n(I) $\\vec{0}$\n(II) $2Gm^2\\hat{j}$\n(III) $-2Gm^2\\hat{j}$\n(IV) $4Gm^2\\hat{j}$","image":"","options":["P-I, Q-II, R-III, S-IV","P-I, Q-III, R-II, S-IV","P-I, Q-II, R-IV, S-III","P-II, Q-I, R-III, S-IV"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(P) By symmetry, the three equal forces at 120° to each other cancel, so the net force is zero. This gives I.\n(Q) Only the extra mass $m$ at A is unbalanced. Its force is $\\frac{G(m)(2m)}{1^2} = 2Gm^2$ towards A. This gives II.\n(R) The forces from B and C add to a resultant equal and opposite to what A would have exerted: $-2Gm^2\\hat{j}$. This gives III.\n(S) The extra $2m$ at A gives $\\frac{G(2m)(2m)}{1} = 4Gm^2$ towards A. This gives IV.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-132","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":132,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"A satellite revolves around the earth in a circular orbit at a height equal to the earth's radius above the surface. Take $R_E = 6400$ km and g = 10 m/s$^2$ at the surface. Its period of revolution is about","image":"","options":["237.0 min","167.6 min","335.1 min","118.5 min"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $r = R_E + h = 2R_E$ and $GM_E = gR_E^2$. \nFormula: $T = 2\\pi\\sqrt{\\frac{r^3}{GM_E}} = 2\\pi\\sqrt{\\frac{8R_E^3}{gR_E^2}} = 2\\pi\\sqrt{\\frac{8R_E}{g}}$ \nSubstituting: $\\sqrt{8\\times6.4\\times10^{6}/10} = 2262.7$ s, so $T = 2\\pi\\times2262.7 \\approx 14217$ s \nSo T ≈ 237.0 min, which is $2\\sqrt{2}$ times the near-surface period. \nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-133","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":133,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"A planet has twice the mass of the Earth and half the Earth's radius. If the escape speed from the Earth's surface is 11.2 km/s, what is the escape speed from the surface of this planet?","image":"","options":["22.4 km/s","11.2 km/s","15.8 km/s","5.6 km/s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $M_p = 2M_E$, $R_p = R_E/2$, $v_E = 11.2$ km/s.\nFormula: $v_e = \\sqrt{\\frac{2GM}{R}}$, so $v_e \\propto \\sqrt{M/R}$.\nSubstituting: $\\frac{v_p}{v_E} = \\sqrt{\\frac{2}{1/2}} = \\sqrt{4} = 2$.\nSo $v_p = 2 \\times 11.2 = 22.4$ km/s.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-134","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":134,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"Different points in earth are at slightly different distances from the sun and hence experience different forces due to gravitation. For a rigid body, we know that if various forces act at various points in it, the resultant motion is as if a net force acts on the c.m. (centre of mass) causing translation and a net torque at the c.m. causing rotation around an axis through the c.m. For the earth-sun system (approximating the earth as a uniform density sphere)","image":"","options":["the torque is zero.","the torque causes the earth to spin.","the rigid body result is not applicable since the earth is not even approximately a rigid body.","the torque causes the earth to move around the sun."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Key fact: A uniform sphere attracts, and is attracted, as if all its mass were at its centre.\nSo the resultant of the sun's pulls on all parts of the earth acts along the line joining the sun and the earth's centre. This line passes through the earth's centre of mass.\nA force through the c.m. has zero moment arm about it, so the torque about the c.m. is zero.\nThe earth's spin is not caused by this torque, so (2) is wrong. Orbital motion comes from the net force, not a torque, so (4) is wrong.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Physics Class 11, Ch 8 (MCQ Q8.3)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-135","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":135,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"Both earth and moon are subject to the gravitational force of the sun. As observed from the sun, the orbit of the moon","image":"","options":["will be elliptical.","will not be strictly elliptical because the total gravitational force on it is not central.","is not elliptical but will necessarily be a closed curve.","deviates considerably from being elliptical due to influence of planets other than earth."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Key fact: A strictly elliptical orbit needs a single central inverse-square force directed towards a fixed point.\nThe moon is pulled by both the sun and the earth. The earth's pull changes direction as the moon goes around it.\nSo the net force on the moon is not always directed towards the sun, which means it is not central.\nHence, as seen from the sun, the moon's orbit is not strictly elliptical. (3) is wrong because nothing guarantees the path is closed. (4) is wrong because other planets have only a tiny effect.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Physics Class 11, Ch 8 (MCQ Q8.5)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-136","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":136,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The total mechanical energy of a satellite in a circular orbit around Earth is negative.\nReason (R): The kinetic energy of an orbiting satellite is equal in magnitude to its gravitational potential energy.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Take the potential energy to be zero at infinity. Then KE = $\\frac{GMm}{2r}$ and PE = $-\\frac{GMm}{r}$.\nA is true. E = KE + PE = $-\\frac{GMm}{2r}$, which is negative. This is consistent with the satellite being bound to Earth.\nR is false. The KE is only half the magnitude of the PE. If they were equal, the total energy would be zero and the body would escape.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-137","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":137,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"A satellite of mass $m$ moves in a circular orbit of radius $r$ around the earth (mass $M$). The potential energy is taken as zero at infinity. Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(A) Kinetic energy\n(B) Potential energy\n(C) Total mechanical energy\n(D) Minimum energy to be supplied for the satellite to escape from the orbit\nList-II\n(I) $-\\frac{GMm}{r}$\n(II) $+\\frac{GMm}{2r}$ (positive kinetic energy)\n(III) $-\\frac{GMm}{2r}$\n(IV) $+\\frac{GMm}{2r}$ (energy to be added)","image":"","options":["A-II, B-I, C-III, D-IV","A-I, B-II, C-III, D-IV","A-II, B-III, C-I, D-IV","A-IV, B-I, C-II, D-III"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) Kinetic energy: from $v^2 = GM/r$, $K = \\frac{GMm}{2r}$, which is (II).\n(B) Potential energy: $U = -\\frac{GMm}{r}$, which is (I).\n(C) Total energy: $E = K + U = -\\frac{GMm}{2r}$, which is (III).\n(D) To escape, the total energy must be raised to zero, so energy $+\\frac{GMm}{2r}$ must be supplied, which is (IV).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-138","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":138,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"A planet moves in an elliptical orbit around the Sun. Its distance from the Sun is $1.4\\times10^{11}$ m at perihelion and $2.1\\times10^{11}$ m at aphelion. Its speed at perihelion is 33 km/s. What is its speed at aphelion?","image":"","options":["49.5 km/s","33 km/s","22 km/s","14.7 km/s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: $r_P = 1.4\\times10^{11}$ m, $r_A = 2.1\\times10^{11}$ m, $v_P$ = 33 km/s.\nFormula: angular momentum is conserved. At P and A the velocity is perpendicular to the radius, so $v_P r_P = v_A r_A$.\nSubstituting: $v_A = 33 \\times \\frac{1.4}{2.1}$.\nSo $v_A$ = 22 km/s. The planet is slower at the farther point.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-139","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":139,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"A planet has 8 times the mass of the Earth and 2 times its radius. At what height above the surface of this planet is the acceleration due to gravity equal to g at the Earth's surface? (Take $R_E$ = 6400 km)","image":"","options":["2650 km","6400 km","5300 km","11700 km"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: $M_p = 8M_E$, $R_p = 2R_E$, $R_E$ = 6400 km.\nFormula: $g_p(h) = \\frac{G(8M_E)}{(2R_E+h)^2}$, and this must equal $\\frac{GM_E}{R_E^2}$.\nSo $(2R_E + h)^2 = 8R_E^2$, which gives $2R_E + h = 2\\sqrt{2}R_E$.\nSubstituting: $h = (2\\sqrt{2} - 2)R_E \\approx 0.828 \\times 6400 \\approx$ 5300 km.\nThe option 11700 km gives the distance from the planet's centre minus $R_E$, not the height above the surface.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-140","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":140,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"A planet moves in an elliptical orbit around the Sun. Its distance from the Sun is $1.4 \\times 10^{11}$ m at perihelion and $2.1 \\times 10^{11}$ m at aphelion. If its speed at perihelion is 30 km/s, its speed at aphelion is:","image":"","options":["20 km/s","45 km/s","30 km/s","13.3 km/s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $r_P = 1.4\\times10^{11}$ m, $r_A = 2.1\\times10^{11}$ m, $v_P$ = 30 km/s.\nFormula: angular momentum is conserved and velocity is perpendicular to r at P and A, so $v_P r_P = v_A r_A$.\nSubstituting: $v_A = \\frac{30 \\times 1.4}{2.1}$.\nSo $v_A$ = 20 km/s. The planet is slower when it is farther from the Sun.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-141","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":141,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"A hypothetical planet moves around the Sun in an orbit whose semi-major axis is 4 times that of the Earth's orbit. What is the length of its year, in Earth years?","image":"","options":["4","8","16","64"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: $a_{planet} = 4a_E$ and $T_E$ = 1 year.\nFormula: by Kepler's law of periods, $T^2 \\propto a^3$, so $\\frac{T}{T_E} = \\left(\\frac{a}{a_E}\\right)^{3/2}$.\nSubstituting: $T = 4^{3/2} \\times 1$ year.\nSo T = 8 years. The value 64 is $T^2$ in units of Earth years squared, not T itself.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-142","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":142,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about escape speed is correct?","image":"","options":["A heavier projectile needs a larger escape speed from the earth's surface than a lighter one","The escape speed from the moon's surface is about 2.3 km/s, so gas molecules faster than this escape, which is why the moon has no atmosphere","The escape speed from a point at height $h$ above the earth is greater than that from the surface","To escape, an object must be projected vertically upward; at any other angle it always falls back"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(1) Wrong: $v_e = \\sqrt{2GM_E/R_E}$ does not depend on the mass of the projectile.\n(2) Correct: this is the NCERT reason the moon has no atmosphere.\n(3) Wrong: from height $h$, $v_{min} = \\sqrt{2GM_E/(R_E+h)}$, which is smaller than the surface value.\n(4) Wrong: the energy condition involves only the speed, not the direction of projection.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-143","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":143,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (person) with List-II (contribution):\nList-I:\n(a) Ptolemy\n(b) Copernicus\n(c) Tycho Brahe\n(d) Johannes Kepler\nList-II:\n(i) Analysed planetary data to extract three laws of planetary motion\n(ii) Geocentric model, with celestial objects moving in circles about the Earth\n(iii) Spent a lifetime recording naked-eye observations of planets\n(iv) Model with planets moving in circles around a fixed central Sun","image":"","options":["(a)-(iv), (b)-(ii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(iii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(a) Ptolemy proposed the geocentric model, so (a)-(ii).\n(b) Copernicus proposed planets moving in circles around a fixed Sun, so (b)-(iv).\n(c) Tycho Brahe compiled naked-eye observations, so (c)-(iii).\n(d) Kepler, Brahe's assistant, analysed that data and obtained the three laws, so (d)-(i).\nThe tempting swap (c)-(i) is wrong: Brahe only collected the data.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-144","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":144,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"A planet of mass m moves around the Sun (mass M, assumed fixed) in an elliptical orbit of semi-major axis a. Its distances from the Sun at perihelion and aphelion are $r_p$ and $r_A$, and its speeds there are $v_p$ and $v_A$. How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) $v_p > v_A$\n(ii) $\\frac{v_p}{v_A} = \\frac{r_A}{r_p}$\n(iii) The kinetic energy of the planet is maximum at perihelion.\n(iv) The total mechanical energy of the planet is $-\\frac{GMm}{2a}$.\n(v) The net work done by the Sun's gravitational force on the planet over one complete revolution is zero.\n(vi) The angular momentum of the planet about the Sun is maximum at perihelion.","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct. The planet moves faster when it is nearer the Sun.\n(ii) Correct. Angular momentum is constant, so $mv_pr_p = mv_Ar_A$.\n(iii) Correct. The speed is largest at perihelion.\n(iv) Correct. For an ellipse, $E = -\\frac{GMm}{2a}$.\n(v) Correct. Gravity is conservative, and the planet returns to its starting point after one revolution.\n(vi) Incorrect. Angular momentum is the same at every point because gravity is a central force.\nSo 5 statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-145","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":145,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"In the equilibrium condition of Cavendish's experiment, $\\frac{GMm}{d^2}L = \\tau\\theta$. Match the symbols in List-I with their meanings in List-II.\nList-I: (A) $\\tau$ (B) $\\theta$ (C) L (D) d\nList-II: (P) Length of the bar AB (Q) Angle of twist of the suspension wire (R) Separation between the centres of a big sphere and its neighbouring small sphere (S) Restoring couple per unit angle of twist","image":"","options":["A-Q, B-S, C-R, D-P","A-S, B-Q, C-R, D-P","A-S, B-Q, C-P, D-R","A-P, B-Q, C-S, D-R"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(A) $\\tau$ is the restoring couple per unit angle of twist of the wire, so A-S.\n(B) $\\theta$ is the angle through which the wire twists, so B-Q.\n(C) L is the length of the bar, and the torque equals F × L, so C-P.\n(D) d is the centre-to-centre distance between a big sphere and its neighbouring small sphere, used in $F = \\frac{GMm}{d^2}$, so D-R.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-146","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":146,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"Two point masses attract each other with a gravitational force F. One of the masses is doubled and the separation between them is tripled. The new force is","image":"","options":["$\\frac{2}{9}F$","$\\frac{2}{3}F$","$6F$","$\\frac{9}{2}F$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $m_1 \\to 2m_1$, $r \\to 3r$\nFormula: $F = \\frac{Gm_1m_2}{r^2}$, so F varies directly with each mass and inversely with $r^2$.\nSubstituting: $F' = \\frac{G(2m_1)m_2}{(3r)^2} = \\frac{2}{9}\\cdot\\frac{Gm_1m_2}{r^2}$\nSo $F' = \\frac{2}{9}F$. The option $\\frac{2}{3}F$ wrongly treats the force as varying as $\\frac{1}{r}$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-147","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":147,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"A point mass m interacts with a body of mass M and radius R. Here r is the distance of m from the centre. Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(A) m outside a uniform thin shell (r > R)\n(B) m inside a uniform thin shell (r < R)\n(C) m inside a uniform solid sphere (r < R)\n(D) m on the surface of a uniform solid sphere\nList-II\n(i) $\\frac{GMm}{R^2}$\n(ii) Zero\n(iii) $\\frac{GMmr}{R^3}$\n(iv) $\\frac{GMm}{r^2}$","image":"","options":["A-(iv), B-(ii), C-(iii), D-(i)","A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(ii), D-(i)","A-(i), B-(ii), C-(iii), D-(iv)","A-(iv), B-(ii), C-(i), D-(iii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) Outside a shell, its mass acts as if at the centre: $\\frac{GMm}{r^2}$, so (iv).\n(B) Inside a shell the force is zero, so (ii).\n(C) Inside a solid sphere, only the mass within radius r acts: $M_r = M\\frac{r^3}{R^3}$, giving $\\frac{GMmr}{R^3}$, so (iii).\n(D) On the surface r = R, giving $\\frac{GMm}{R^2}$, so (i).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-148","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":148,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I:\n(A) Law of orbits\n(B) Law of areas\n(C) Law of periods\n(D) Aphelion\nList-II:\n(I) Square of the time period is proportional to the cube of the semi-major axis\n(II) Point of the orbit farthest from the Sun\n(III) Sun is located at one focus of the elliptical path\n(IV) Follows from conservation of angular momentum under a central force","image":"","options":["A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II","A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II","A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) Law of orbits: planets move in ellipses with the Sun at one focus, so A matches (III).\n(B) Law of areas: equal areas are swept in equal times, which follows from conservation of angular momentum, so B matches (IV).\n(C) Law of periods: $T^2 \\propto a^3$, so C matches (I).\n(D) Aphelion is the farthest point from the Sun, so D matches (II).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-149","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":149,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"The acceleration due to gravity at the earth's surface is $10$ m/s$^2$ and the radius of the earth is $6400$ km. Using the approximation valid for small heights, what is the acceleration due to gravity at a height of $32$ km above the surface?","image":"","options":["$9.9$ m/s$^2$","$9.95$ m/s$^2$","$9.8$ m/s$^2$","$9.0$ m/s$^2$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $g = 10$ m/s$^2$, $h = 32$ km, $R_E = 6400$ km, so $h \\ll R_E$.\nFormula: $g(h) \\approx g\\left(1 - \\frac{2h}{R_E}\\right)$\nSubstituting: $g(h) = 10\\left(1 - \\frac{64}{6400}\\right) = 10(1 - 0.01)$\nSo $g(h) = 9.9$ m/s$^2$.\nNote: $9.95$ m/s$^2$ is what you get if you wrongly drop the factor 2, using $(1 - h/R_E)$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-150","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":150,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Using $g(h) = g\\left(1 - \\frac{2h}{R_E}\\right)$ at a height $h = \\frac{R_E}{2}$ gives g(h) = 0. However, the actual value there is $\\frac{4g}{9}$.\nReason (R): The expression $g\\left(1 - \\frac{2h}{R_E}\\right)$ is obtained from a binomial expansion of $(1 + h/R_E)^{-2}$, which is valid only when $h \\ll R_E$.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A: Putting $h = R_E/2$ in the approximate formula gives $g(1 - 1) = 0$.\nThe exact formula gives $g\\left(\\frac{R_E}{1.5R_E}\\right)^2 = \\frac{4g}{9}$. So A is true.\nR: The approximation keeps only the first-order term of $(1 + h/R_E)^{-2}$. This is justified only for $h \\ll R_E$, so R is true.\nThe failure of the approximation at large h is exactly why A happens. So R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-151","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":151,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The currently accepted value of the gravitational constant is $G = 6.67\\times10^{-11}$ N m$^2$ kg$^{-2}$.\nStatement II: Measuring G and combining it with the known values of g and $R_E$ allows the mass of the earth to be estimated.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I: Correct. NCERT gives $G = 6.67\\times10^{-11}$ N m$^2$ kg$^{-2}$.\nStatement II: Correct. From $g = \\frac{GM_E}{R_E^2}$ we get $M_E = \\frac{gR_E^2}{G}$, so g, $R_E$ and G together give $M_E$.\nThis is why people say that Cavendish 'weighed the earth'.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-152","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":152,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"Two uniform solid spheres, each of radius R, have masses M and 9M. Their centres are 10R apart and both spheres are held fixed. A small projectile is fired from the surface of the lighter sphere straight towards the centre of the heavier one. What is the minimum launch speed for it to reach the surface of the heavier sphere?","image":"","options":["$\\sqrt{\\frac{4GM}{5R}}$","$\\sqrt{\\frac{2GM}{R}}$","$\\sqrt{\\frac{3GM}{5R}}$","$\\sqrt{\\frac{8GM}{5R}}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The projectile only needs to reach the neutral point N. Beyond N, the pull of 9M brings it the rest of the way.\nNeutral point: $\\frac{GM}{r^2} = \\frac{9GM}{(10R-r)^2}$, so $10R - r = 3r$ and r = 2.5R from the centre of M.\nEnergy per unit mass at launch: $-\\frac{GM}{R} - \\frac{9GM}{9R} = -\\frac{2GM}{R}$.\nEnergy per unit mass at N: $-\\frac{GM}{2.5R} - \\frac{9GM}{7.5R} = -\\frac{1.6GM}{R}$.\nEnergy conservation: $\\frac{1}{2}v^2 = \\frac{0.4GM}{R}$, so $v = \\sqrt{\\frac{4GM}{5R}}$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-153","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":153,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"In vector form, the gravitational force on point mass $m_2$ due to point mass $m_1$ is written as $\\vec{F} = -\\frac{Gm_1m_2}{r^2}\\hat{r}$, where $\\hat{r}$ is the unit vector pointing from $m_1$ to $m_2$. The negative sign indicates that","image":"","options":["the force on $m_2$ is directed from $m_2$ towards $m_1$, i.e. the force is attractive","the force on $m_2$ is directed away from $m_1$, i.e. the force is repulsive","the gravitational potential energy of the pair is always negative","the magnitude of the force decreases as $r$ increases"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"$\\hat{r}$ points from $m_1$ to $m_2$, so $-\\hat{r}$ points from $m_2$ back towards $m_1$.\nThe force on $m_2$ therefore pulls it towards $m_1$. This is what makes gravity attractive.\nOption (3) is a separate fact about potential energy. It is not what the sign in the force law shows.\nOption (4) comes from the $\\frac{1}{r^2}$ factor, not from the sign.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-154","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":154,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"A satellite of mass 1000 kg is to be placed in a circular orbit at a height equal to the earth's radius above the surface. Find the minimum energy that must be supplied to it on the earth's surface to do so. Ignore the earth's rotation and air resistance. Take $R_E = 6.4 \\times 10^{6}$ m and g = 10 m/s$^2$.","image":"","options":["$1.6 \\times 10^{10}$ J","$3.2 \\times 10^{10}$ J","$4.8 \\times 10^{10}$ J","$6.4 \\times 10^{10}$ J"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: m = 1000 kg, orbit radius r = $2R_E$, $GM = gR_E^2$.\nOn the surface (at rest): $E_i = -\\frac{GMm}{R_E}$. In orbit: $E_f = -\\frac{GMm}{2(2R_E)} = -\\frac{GMm}{4R_E}$.\nFormula: energy needed $= E_f - E_i = \\frac{3GMm}{4R_E} = \\frac{3}{4}gR_E m$.\nSubstituting: $0.75 \\times 10 \\times 6.4 \\times 10^{6} \\times 1000$.\nSo the energy = $4.8 \\times 10^{10}$ J. Option (1) is only the energy needed to escape from the orbit, $\\frac{GMm}{4R_E}$.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-155","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":155,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"A satellite revolves in a circular orbit just above the surface of the earth, so its height can be neglected. Take $g = 10$ m/s$^2$ and $R_E = 6.4 \\times 10^{6}$ m. What is its orbital speed?","image":"","options":["8.0 km/s","11.3 km/s","5.66 km/s","4.0 km/s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $h \\approx 0$, $g = 10$ m/s$^2$, $R_E = 6.4 \\times 10^6$ m.\nFormula: $V^2 = \\frac{GM_E}{R_E} = gR_E$, so $V = \\sqrt{gR_E}$.\nSubstituting: $V = \\sqrt{10 \\times 6.4 \\times 10^6} = \\sqrt{6.4 \\times 10^7} = 8000$ m/s.\nSo the orbital speed is 8.0 km/s. The value 11.3 km/s is $\\sqrt{2gR_E}$, the escape speed, not the orbital speed.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-156","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":156,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: A planet having the same mean density as the earth but twice its radius has twice the earth's surface value of g.\nStatement II: A planet having the same mass as the earth but twice its radius has half the earth's surface value of g.","image":"","options":["Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both statements are correct","Both statements are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Start from $g = \\frac{GM}{R^2}$ with $M = \\frac{4}{3}\\pi R^3\\rho$, which gives $g = \\frac{4}{3}\\pi G\\rho R$.\nStatement I is correct: at equal density, g ∝ R, so doubling R doubles g.\nStatement II is incorrect: at equal mass, g ∝ $\\frac{1}{R^2}$, so doubling R makes g one-fourth, not one-half.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-157","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":157,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) A hollow spherical shell placed around a particle shields it from the gravitational pull of bodies outside the shell.\n(ii) The linear momentum of a planet moving around the Sun is conserved.\n(iii) The angular momentum of a planet about the Sun is conserved.\n(iv) The gravitational force between two finite rigid bodies always acts along the line joining their centres of mass.\n(v) The expression $mgh$ for potential energy is an approximation to the difference in gravitational potential energy.","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Incorrect: the shell exerts no force on a particle inside it, but it does not block other bodies; gravitational shielding is not possible.\n(ii) Incorrect: the planet's velocity keeps changing direction, so linear momentum is not conserved.\n(iii) Correct: gravity is a central force, so angular momentum is conserved.\n(iv) Incorrect: this holds only for spherically symmetric bodies, not for finite rigid bodies in general.\n(v) Correct: NCERT states this.\nSo 2 statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-158","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":158,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The mass of the earth can be determined from the moon's orbital radius and period of revolution, even if the mass of the moon is not known.\nReason (R): In the relation $T^2 = \\frac{4\\pi^2 R^3}{GM_E}$ for a satellite, the mass of the satellite cancels out.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: NCERT 'weighs the earth' using $M_E = \\frac{4\\pi^2R^3}{GT^2}$ with the moon's distance and period. This gives about $6\\times10^{24}$ kg, within 1% of the value from $M_E = gR_E^2/G$. \nR is true: when $\\frac{mv^2}{R}$ is equated to $\\frac{GmM_E}{R^2}$, the satellite mass m cancels. \nThis cancellation is exactly why the moon's mass is not needed, so R explains A. \nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-159","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":159,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): With the zero of gravitational potential energy chosen at infinity, the potential energy of a body and the earth is negative at every finite separation.\nReason (R): Gravity is attractive, so it does positive work on the body as the body is brought in from infinity. This lowers the potential energy below its value at infinity.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: $U = -\\frac{GMm}{r}$ is negative for any finite $r$ when $U(\\infty)=0$.\nR is true: an attractive force does positive work as the separation decreases, so the potential energy decreases.\nSince $U$ starts at zero at infinity, it must become negative. R therefore explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-160","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":160,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"An ellipse is drawn by fixing the two ends of a string at pins $F_1$ and $F_2$ and moving a pencil tip so that the string stays taut. Which of the following statements is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["For every point on the curve, the sum of its distances from $F_1$ and $F_2$ is the same.","The midpoint of the line joining the perihelion and the aphelion is the centre of the ellipse.","When the two foci merge into one, the ellipse becomes a circle and the semi-major axis becomes its radius.","In a planet's orbit, the Sun lies at the centre of the ellipse, midway between the two foci."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: the string length is fixed, so the sum of the distances from the two foci is constant.\n(2) Correct: O, the midpoint of PA, is the centre, and PO = AO is the semi-major axis.\n(3) Correct: a circle is the special case in which the foci coincide.\n(4) Incorrect: by Kepler's law of orbits, the Sun is at one focus, not at the centre.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-161","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":161,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"A comet moves around the Sun in an elliptical orbit. Its closest distance from the Sun is 1 AU and its farthest distance is 7 AU. The Earth's orbit is nearly circular with radius 1 AU and period 1 year. The orbital period of the comet is:","image":"","options":["4 years","18.5 years","8 years","2.83 years"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: $r_P$ = 1 AU and $r_A$ = 7 AU.\nThe semi-major axis is half of AP: $a = \\frac{r_P + r_A}{2} = \\frac{1 + 7}{2}$ = 4 AU.\nFormula (law of periods): $T^2 \\propto a^3$, so $T = T_E \\left(\\frac{a}{a_E}\\right)^{3/2}$.\nSubstituting: $T = 1 \\times 4^{3/2}$ = 8 years.\nUsing the aphelion distance 7 AU instead of a wrongly gives 18.5 years.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-162","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":162,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II ($R_E$ = radius of Earth, $g$ = acceleration due to gravity at the surface).\nList-I\n(a) Escape speed from Earth's surface\n(b) Orbital speed of a satellite just above Earth's surface\n(c) Time period of a satellite just above Earth's surface\n(d) Acceleration due to gravity at depth $d$\nList-II\n(i) $2\\pi\\sqrt{\\frac{R_E}{g}}$\n(ii) $\\sqrt{2gR_E}$\n(iii) $g\\left(1-\\frac{d}{R_E}\\right)$\n(iv) $\\sqrt{gR_E}$","image":"","options":["(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(ii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(iii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iii), (d)-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) Escape speed: $v_e=\\sqrt{2GM_E/R_E}=\\sqrt{2gR_E}$, which is (ii).\n(b) Orbital speed at h = 0: $V^2=GM_E/R_E=gR_E$, so $V=\\sqrt{gR_E}$, which is (iv).\n(c) Period: $T_0=2\\pi R_E/V=2\\pi\\sqrt{R_E/g}$, which is (i).\n(d) g at depth d: $g(1-d/R_E)$, which is (iii).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-163","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":163,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"As observed from earth, the sun appears to move in an approximate circular orbit. For the motion of another planet like mercury as observed from earth, this would","image":"","options":["be similarly true.","not be true because the force between earth and mercury is not inverse square law.","not be true because the major gravitational force on mercury is due to sun.","not be true because mercury is influenced by forces other than gravitational forces."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Key fact: Each planet, including Mercury, is held in orbit mainly by the sun's gravity.\nThe sun appears to circle the earth only because of the earth's own orbital motion. That is simply the relative view of the earth–sun pair.\nMercury orbits the sun, not the earth. Seen from the moving earth, its path looks complicated, with loops and retrograde motion, rather than a simple circle.\n(2) is wrong because the earth–Mercury force does follow the inverse-square law. (4) is wrong because only gravity matters here.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Physics Class 11, Ch 8 (MCQ Q8.2)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-164","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":164,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"In a Cavendish-type apparatus, a big lead sphere of mass 100 kg and a small lead sphere of mass 1.0 kg have a centre-to-centre separation of 0.10 m. Find the gravitational force between them. Take $G = 6.67\\times10^{-11}$ N m$^2$ kg$^{-2}$.","image":"","options":["$6.67\\times10^{-8}$ N","$3.34\\times10^{-7}$ N","$6.67\\times10^{-6}$ N","$6.67\\times10^{-7}$ N"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Given: M = 100 kg, m = 1.0 kg, d = 0.10 m.\nFormula: For spheres, the masses act as if concentrated at the centres, so $F = \\frac{GMm}{d^2}$.\nSubstituting: $F = \\frac{6.67\\times10^{-11}\\times100\\times1}{(0.10)^2} = 6.67\\times10^{-7}$ N.\nSo F = $6.67\\times10^{-7}$ N. Using d instead of $d^2$ would give $6.67\\times10^{-8}$ N, which is wrong.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-165","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":165,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"For satellites of the earth, Kepler's law of periods is $T^2 = kr^3$ with $k = 10^{-13}$ s$^2$ m$^{-3}$, where r is the orbital radius measured from the earth's centre. A satellite moves in a circular orbit of radius $4.2\\times10^{4}$ km. Its period is closest to","image":"","options":["23.9 h","8.4 h","47.8 h","0.996 h"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $r = 4.2\\times10^{4}$ km $= 4.2\\times10^{7}$ m (k is in SI units, so convert km to m). \nFormula: $T = \\sqrt{kr^3}$ \nSubstituting: $r^3 = 7.41\\times10^{22}$ m$^3$, so $T^2 = 7.41\\times10^{9}$ s$^2$ and $T \\approx 8.61\\times10^{4}$ s. \nSo $T \\approx 23.9$ h, which is about the geostationary period. \nOption (4) wrongly writes about 1 day as 0.996 h. \nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-166","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":166,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"Taking g = 10 m/s$^2$ at the surface and $R_E$ = 6400 km, use the approximation valid for $h \\ll R_E$ to find the acceleration due to gravity at a height of 32 km.","image":"","options":["9.0 m/s$^2$","9.8 m/s$^2$","9.95 m/s$^2$","9.9 m/s$^2$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Given: g = 10 m/s$^2$, h = 32 km, $R_E$ = 6400 km.\nFormula: $g(h) \\approx g\\left(1 - \\frac{2h}{R_E}\\right)$.\nSubstituting: $\\frac{2h}{R_E} = \\frac{64}{6400} = 0.01$, so g(h) = 10(1 − 0.01).\nSo g(h) = 9.9 m/s$^2$. The value 9.95 m/s$^2$ comes from dropping the factor 2.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-167","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":167,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"At a small height h = 5 km above the earth's surface, the acceleration due to gravity has a certain value. At what depth below the surface does g have the same value? Assume h << $R_E$ and a uniform earth.","image":"","options":["10 km","5 km","2.5 km","20 km"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: h = 5 km, with h << $R_E$.\nFormula: at height, $g(h) \\approx g\\left(1 - \\frac{2h}{R_E}\\right)$; at depth, $g(d) = g\\left(1 - \\frac{d}{R_E}\\right)$.\nSetting them equal gives $\\frac{d}{R_E} = \\frac{2h}{R_E}$, so d = 2h.\nSo d = 2 × 5 = 10 km.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-168","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":168,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"Newton compared the centripetal acceleration of the moon with the acceleration due to gravity g at the earth's surface. Take the radius of the moon's orbit as 60 times the earth's radius and $g = 9.8$ m/s$^2$. Assuming the inverse-square law, what is the centripetal acceleration of the moon?","image":"","options":["$2.72 \\times 10^{-3}$ m/s$^2$","$1.63 \\times 10^{-1}$ m/s$^2$","$4.54 \\times 10^{-5}$ m/s$^2$","$2.72 \\times 10^{-2}$ m/s$^2$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $R_m = 60R_E$, $g = 9.8$ m/s$^2$\nFormula: the inverse-square law gives $\\frac{a_m}{g} = \\left(\\frac{R_E}{R_m}\\right)^2$\nSubstituting: $a_m = \\frac{9.8}{60^2} = \\frac{9.8}{3600}$\nSo $a_m \\approx 2.72 \\times 10^{-3}$ m/s$^2$. This agrees with $\\frac{4\\pi^2R_m}{T^2}$ for T = 27.3 days.\nThe value $1.63 \\times 10^{-1}$ m/s$^2$ wrongly uses $\\frac{g}{60}$ instead of $\\frac{g}{60^2}$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-169","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":169,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"In the vector form of Newton's law of gravitation, the force on mass $m_2$ due to mass $m_1$ is written as $\\vec{F} = -\\frac{Gm_1m_2}{r^2}\\hat{r}$, where $\\hat{r}$ is the unit vector pointing from $m_1$ to $m_2$. What does the negative sign indicate?","image":"","options":["The force on $m_2$ points towards $m_1$, so the force is attractive","The force on $m_2$ points away from $m_1$, so the force is repulsive","The force decreases as the distance increases","The gravitational potential energy is negative"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"$\\hat{r}$ points from $m_1$ towards $m_2$.\nThe minus sign reverses this direction, so the force on $m_2$ points back towards $m_1$. This means gravity is attractive.\nThe decrease of force with distance comes from the $\\frac{1}{r^2}$ factor, not from the sign.\nThe sign of the potential energy is a separate idea. The negative sign here describes the direction of the force.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-170","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":170,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about gravitational forces are correct?\n(i) The gravitational force between two point masses acts along the line joining them.\n(ii) The gravitational force between two finite rigid bodies always acts along the line joining their centres of mass.\n(iii) The net force on a point mass due to several others is the vector sum of the individual forces, each calculated as if the others were absent.\n(iv) The gravitational forces that two particles exert on each other are equal in magnitude and opposite in direction.","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: the force between point masses is central.\n(ii) Incorrect: NCERT notes the force between finite rigid bodies is not necessarily along the line joining their centres of mass. It is central only for spherically symmetric bodies acting on external particles.\n(iii) Correct: this is the principle of superposition.\n(iv) Correct: the pair obeys Newton's third law.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-171","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":171,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"Treat the earth as a sphere of uniform density with radius $R_E$. A point mass m is placed inside the earth at a distance r from the centre ($r < R_E$). How does the magnitude of the gravitational force on m vary with r?","image":"","options":["It is directly proportional to r","It is inversely proportional to $r^2$","It is independent of r","It is inversely proportional to r"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The shells of radius greater than r exert no net force on m, because m lies inside them.\nThe inner sphere of radius r has mass $M_r = \\frac{4}{3}\\pi \\rho r^3$, which is proportional to $r^3$.\nThis inner sphere acts as if all of $M_r$ were at the centre, so $F = \\frac{G m M_r}{r^2} \\propto \\frac{r^3}{r^2} = r$.\nThe $\\frac{1}{r^2}$ law holds only outside the earth, so option (2) fails here.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-172","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":172,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"For a satellite moving in a circular orbit around the earth, what is the ratio of its kinetic energy to its potential energy to its total mechanical energy (KE : PE : E)? Take the potential energy at infinity as zero.","image":"","options":["1 : 2 : 1","1 : −2 : −1","1 : −1 : 0","2 : −1 : 1"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"For a circular orbit of radius r: KE = $\\frac{GMm}{2r}$.\nPE = $-\\frac{GMm}{r}$, which is twice the KE in magnitude and negative.\nE = KE + PE = $-\\frac{GMm}{2r}$.\nSo KE : PE : E = 1 : −2 : −1.\nOption (3) wrongly treats PE as equal in magnitude to KE, which would make the total energy zero. That would mean the satellite is not bound.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-173","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":173,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The line joining a planet to the Sun sweeps out equal areas in equal intervals of time.\nReason (R): The conservation of angular momentum that leads to this result holds only for forces that vary as the inverse square of distance.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true: it is Kepler's law of areas.\nR is false: NCERT notes that angular momentum conservation, which leads to the law of areas, is not special to the inverse square law.\nAngular momentum is conserved for any central force, because a central force exerts zero torque about the centre.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-174","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":174,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"Two point masses of 100 kg and 50 kg are kept 1.0 m apart. Take the potential energy to be zero at infinite separation and $G = 6.67\\times10^{-11}$ N m$^2$ kg$^{-2}$. What is the gravitational potential energy of the pair?","image":"","options":["$-3.34\\times10^{-7}$ J","$+3.34\\times10^{-7}$ J","$-6.67\\times10^{-7}$ J","$-1.67\\times10^{-7}$ J"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $m_1 = 100$ kg, $m_2 = 50$ kg, $r = 1.0$ m.\nFormula: $U = -\\frac{Gm_1m_2}{r}$, with zero at infinity.\nSubstituting: $U = -\\frac{6.67\\times10^{-11}\\times100\\times50}{1}$.\nSo $U \\approx -3.34\\times10^{-7}$ J. The value is negative because the force is attractive.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-175","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":175,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"A 600 kg satellite moves in a circular orbit of radius $2R$ about the Earth, where R = $6.4 \\times 10^{6}$ m is the Earth's radius. Take g = 10 m/s$^2$ at the surface. What minimum energy must be supplied to shift it to a circular orbit of radius $3R$?","image":"","options":["$3.2 \\times 10^{9}$ J","$6.4 \\times 10^{9}$ J","$9.6 \\times 10^{9}$ J","$1.6 \\times 10^{9}$ J"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: m = 600 kg, $GM = gR^2$, orbit radius changes from 2R to 3R.\nFormula: $E = -\\frac{GMm}{2r} = -\\frac{mgR^2}{2r}$.\nSubstituting: $E_i = -\\frac{mgR}{4}$ and $E_f = -\\frac{mgR}{6}$, so $\\Delta E = \\frac{mgR}{12}$.\n$\\Delta E = \\frac{600 \\times 10 \\times 6.4\\times10^6}{12} = 3.2\\times10^9$ J.\nTrap: PE rises by $mgR/6 = 6.4\\times10^9$ J, but KE falls by $3.2\\times10^9$ J, so the net energy needed is smaller.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-176","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":176,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"Match the height h above the earth's surface (List-I) with the acceleration due to gravity there (List-II), where g is the surface value and $R_E$ the earth's radius.\nList-I:\n(A) $h = R_E$\n(B) $h = 2R_E$\n(C) $h = 3R_E$\n(D) $h = (\\sqrt{2}-1)R_E$\nList-II:\n(i) $g/2$\n(ii) $g/16$\n(iii) $g/4$\n(iv) $g/9$","image":"","options":["A-(iii), B-(ii), C-(iv), D-(i)","A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(ii), D-(i)","A-(i), B-(iv), C-(ii), D-(iii)","A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(i), D-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Use $g(h) = g\\left(\\frac{R_E}{R_E+h}\\right)^2$.\n(A) $R_E + h = 2R_E$, giving $\\frac{g}{4}$, so (iii).\n(B) $R_E + h = 3R_E$, giving $\\frac{g}{9}$, so (iv).\n(C) $R_E + h = 4R_E$, giving $\\frac{g}{16}$, so (ii).\n(D) $R_E + h = \\sqrt{2}R_E$, giving $\\frac{g}{2}$, so (i).\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-177","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":177,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"An asteroid moves around the Sun in a nearly circular orbit of radius 2.25 times the Earth's orbital radius. What is the period of the asteroid's revolution in Earth years?","image":"","options":["3.375 years","2.25 years","5.06 years","1.5 years"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $R_A=2.25\\,R_E$ and $T_E=1$ year.\nFormula: Kepler's third law, $T^2\\propto R^3$, so $\\frac{T_A}{T_E}=\\left(\\frac{R_A}{R_E}\\right)^{3/2}$.\nSubstituting: $T_A=(2.25)^{3/2}=(1.5)^3$ years.\nSo $T_A=3.375$ years.\nThe option 5.06 years wrongly uses $T\\propto R^2$, and 1.5 years wrongly uses $T\\propto R^{1/2}$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-178","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":178,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"A small body is placed inside a uniform hollow spherical shell, and the shell is kept near a massive planet. The net gravitational force on the small body (ignoring all other bodies) is:","image":"","options":["zero, because the shell cancels all gravitational forces inside it","equal to the planet's pull on the body, the same as if the shell were absent","only the shell's pull on the body, since the planet's pull is screened","the planet's pull reduced in proportion to the shell's mass"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The shell exerts zero net force on a body inside it.\nHowever, unlike a metallic shell that shields electric forces, a mass shell does not block the pull of outside bodies. Gravitational shielding is not possible.\nSo the planet still pulls the body exactly as it would without the shell.\nThe tempting option 'zero' wrongly assumes that the shell also cancels external forces.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-179","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":179,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"A body is projected from the Earth's surface with a speed equal to twice the escape speed (escape speed = 11.2 km/s). Ignoring the Sun, the other planets and air resistance, what is its speed when it is very far away from the Earth?","image":"","options":["22.4 km/s","19.4 km/s","11.2 km/s","25.0 km/s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: $v_i = 2v_e$, with $v_e = 11.2$ km/s.\nFormula (energy conservation): $\\frac{1}{2}mv_i^2 - \\frac{GM_Em}{R_E} = \\frac{1}{2}mv_f^2$. Since $\\frac{GM_E}{R_E} = \\frac{v_e^2}{2}$, this gives $v_f^2 = v_i^2 - v_e^2$.\nSubstituting: $v_f^2 = 4v_e^2 - v_e^2 = 3v_e^2$, so $v_f = \\sqrt{3} \\times 11.2$.\nSo $v_f \\approx 19.4$ km/s.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-180","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":180,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"A small moon revolves around a distant planet in a circular orbit of radius $1.0\\times10^{7}$ m with a period of $2.0\\times10^{4}$ s. Taking $G = 6.67\\times10^{-11}$ N m$^2$/kg$^2$, the mass of the planet is about","image":"","options":["$1.48\\times10^{24}$ kg","$2.96\\times10^{24}$ kg","$7.40\\times10^{23}$ kg","$5.92\\times10^{24}$ kg"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: R = $1.0\\times10^{7}$ m, T = $2.0\\times10^{4}$ s. \nFormula: $M = \\frac{4\\pi^2R^3}{GT^2}$ \nSubstituting: numerator $= 39.48\\times10^{21}$; denominator $= 6.67\\times10^{-11}\\times4\\times10^{8} = 2.668\\times10^{-2}$ \nSo $M \\approx 1.48\\times10^{24}$ kg. \nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-181","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":181,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"An astronaut inside a spacecraft orbiting the earth at a height of a few hundred kilometres feels weightless. According to NCERT, the correct reason is that:","image":"","options":["the gravitational force of the earth at that height is negligibly small","the astronaut and the satellite are both in free fall towards the earth","the centrifugal force of the orbit exactly balances gravity on the astronaut","the spacecraft walls shield the astronaut from the earth's gravity"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT (Points to Ponder) says weightlessness is not caused by gravity being small at that location.\nThe astronaut and the satellite are both in free fall towards the earth with the same acceleration, so the floor pushes on the astronaut with no force.\nOption (1) is tempting but wrong: at a few hundred km, g is only slightly less than its surface value.\nOption (4) is wrong because gravitational shielding is not possible.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-GRAVITATION-182","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Gravitation","qno":182,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Gravitation","type":"mcq","question":"Using the approximation valid for small heights, at what height above the Earth's surface does the acceleration due to gravity become 1% less than its value at the surface? (Take $R_E$ = 6400 km)","image":"","options":["64 km","32 km","16 km","3.2 km"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: g(h) = 0.99 g and $R_E$ = 6400 km.\nFormula: for $h \\ll R_E$, $g(h) \\approx g\\left(1 - \\frac{2h}{R_E}\\right)$.\nSo the fractional decrease is $\\frac{2h}{R_E} = 0.01$.\nSubstituting: $h = \\frac{0.01 \\times 6400}{2}$ km = 32 km.\nThe option 64 km comes from forgetting the factor 2.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 7 · Gravitation","difficulty":"medium"}]});
